window.RMSStudentGuidance={"version":"2.1","glossary":{"analysis":"The process of organising evidence to address a research question. Analysis may involve describing observations, using statistical methods, interpreting qualitative records or synthesising findings across studies.","association":"A pattern in which two variables vary together. An association by itself does not show that one variable caused the other.","attrition":"Loss of participants or units after a study begins. If attrition differs across conditions or types of participants, it can change the interpretation.","binary":"A variable with exactly two possible categories for the analysis, such as germinated/not germinated, correct/incorrect, or yes/no. Binary describes the number of categories, not whether the variable is numerical.","blinding":"Keeping a person from knowing information that could influence behaviour, measurement, or scoring, such as which treatment a sample received.","blocking":"Grouping similar experimental units before random assignment, then assigning conditions within those groups. This can reduce imbalance on an important characteristic.","boolean search":"A search using operators such as AND and OR to combine concepts. OR groups alternative terms for a concept, including related terms when justified; AND combines different concept groups. Check how each database interprets the syntax.","calibration":"Comparing an instrument’s readings with an appropriate reference standard to assess measurement error, and adjusting the instrument when needed.","categorical":"Data that place observations into named groups or categories. Examples include habitat type, treatment group, blood type, and species. Arithmetic on the category names is usually meaningless.","causal claim":"A claim that changing one factor produces a change in an outcome. Strong causal claims need a design that addresses alternative explanations, not merely a statistically significant result.","chi-square test":"A family of tests using observed and expected counts, including goodness-of-fit tests and tests of association between categorical variables. The expected counts and assumptions matter; percentages cannot be substituted for raw counts.","coding":"Applying explicit labels or rules to records. In qualitative research, coding can involve labelling passages of text or other materials; in quantitative data, it can specify how categories such as yes and no are represented.","confidence interval":"A range calculated from data using a method intended to capture the target parameter at a stated rate over repeated studies, provided its assumptions hold. A 95% confidence interval does not give a 95% probability that the fixed parameter lies within this particular interval.","confounder":"A factor that can influence both exposure or group membership and the outcome, potentially distorting their observed relationship. Statistical association with both does not alone establish confounding.","construct":"An idea you want to study that is not yet a direct measurement, such as stress, memory, biodiversity, engagement, or trust.","continuous":"A numerical variable that can, in principle, take many values across an interval, such as temperature, mass, time, or concentration. Real instruments may record it to limited precision.","control condition":"A comparison condition used as a reference. It is different from controlled conditions, which are procedures or environmental features kept similar.","controlled condition":"A feature deliberately standardised across study conditions so that differences in that feature are less likely to explain an observed contrast. Examples include the same measurement time, solution volume or scoring procedure.","correlation":"A numerical description of the direction and strength of association between two variables. Correlation does not by itself establish causation.","counterbalancing":"Varying the order of conditions across participants or units so a particular condition does not always come first. It can distribute or help reveal order, practice and fatigue effects; carryover may still require additional design safeguards.","covariate":"A measured variable included because it may explain outcome variation, improve precision, or address a known source of imbalance. Whether statistical adjustment is appropriate depends on the design and causal question.","data dictionary":"A document that explains every dataset column, what it means, its type, units or categories, coding rules, and how missing values are recorded.","dependent variable":"A common experimental term for the measured outcome expected to respond to the manipulated independent variable.","descriptive statistics":"Numerical summaries of observed data, such as mean, median, standard deviation, interquartile range, counts, and proportions.","effect size":"A quantitative description of the magnitude of a difference or association. A small p-value does not automatically mean a large effect.","estimand":"The quantity a quantitative study aims to estimate, defined by the target population, outcome, comparison or condition and relevant time or measurement rule. A mean difference, proportion or correlation can be an estimand when these details are specified. The numerical value calculated from data is an estimate.","experimental unit":"In an experiment, the smallest unit independently assigned to a treatment condition. Multiple measurements or subsamples within that unit do not automatically constitute independent treatment replicates.","exposure":"A condition, behaviour, characteristic or environmental factor whose relationship with an outcome is examined. In observational research, exposure is recorded rather than assigned by the researcher; an experimentally assigned factor is usually described as a treatment.","external validity":"How well a study's conclusions may apply beyond the specific units, setting, measurements, and conditions studied.","Fisher exact test":"An exact test for association in a 2×2 categorical table. It is often considered when counts are sparse and chi-square approximations may be questionable.","generalization":"Extending a conclusion from the studied units or setting to a wider population or context. Whether that generalisation is warranted depends on how units were selected, what was measured and how the study was conducted.","hypothesis":"A specific prediction that can be evaluated with evidence. A formal hypothesis is useful for many confirmatory quantitative studies but is not required for every research design.","identifier":"A code or label, such as plant_01 or participant_A17, used to link records to the same unit across measurements or nesting levels. Coded identifiers still require appropriate privacy protection when a person could be re-identified.","independent groups":"Groups made up of distinct, unpaired units. Repeated observations on the same unit, matched pairs and measurements clustered within the same classroom or dish can introduce dependence that the analysis must address.","independent variable":"In an experiment, the factor deliberately manipulated by the researcher. A measured predictor in an observational study should usually be called a predictor or exposure, not a manipulated independent variable.","independence":"A statistical/design condition concerning whether observations provide separate information. Repeated measurements from the same unit, students in one classroom, or subsamples from one specimen are often dependent.","inference":"A conclusion that goes beyond simply listing the observed data. Statistical inference uses a model and assumptions; scientific inference also depends on the research design.","interquartile range":"The distance between the 25th and 75th percentiles. It summarizes the spread of the middle half of numerical data and is often abbreviated IQR.","interval estimate":"An estimated range for a target population or model parameter, accompanied by a method-specific account of uncertainty. A confidence interval is one example; it is different from the range of individual observations.","literature gap":"An issue, context or question that has not been adequately addressed within the relevant literature. A defensible gap claim requires a documented search and synthesis appropriate to its scope; a brief scan cannot establish a universal absence of research.","mean":"The arithmetic average. It is useful for many numerical variables, but it can be influenced by extreme values and may not describe a strongly skewed distribution well by itself.","measurement validity":"Evidence supporting the interpretation you want to make from a measurement or score. A reliable measure can still measure the wrong thing.","median":"After numerical observations are ordered, the median is the middle value for an odd number of observations, or the mean of the two middle values for an even number. It can be useful for skewed distributions or when extreme values strongly affect the mean.","meta-analysis":"A statistical synthesis of results from multiple studies when their questions and effect estimates can be meaningfully combined. The analysis must consider differences among studies, sources of bias and any dependence between estimates.","missing data":"An intended observation or value that was not obtained or recorded. Missingness may affect interpretation, and an unknown value must remain distinct from an observed zero or a category that did not apply.","moderator":"A variable associated with a difference in the size or direction of a relationship across groups or conditions.","nominal":"Categorical data with no inherent ranking, such as species, blood type, or classroom section.","null hypothesis":"A formal statistical hypothesis used by an inferential test, often representing no difference, no association, or a specified parameter value under the model. It is not automatically the researcher's true scientific belief.","numerical":"Data representing quantities for which arithmetic has a substantive interpretation, such as height, elapsed time or concentration. Numerical-looking category codes, such as 1 for one group and 2 for another, remain categorical data.","observational study":"A study in which the researcher measures variables or exposures without assigning the key exposure of interest.","observational unit":"The unit on which variables are observed or recorded. It may or may not be the same as the experimental unit in an experiment.","operational definition":"The exact rule for how a construct will be manipulated, measured, counted, scored, coded, or observed.","ordinal":"Categories with a meaningful order but without guaranteed equal spacing between levels, such as low/medium/high or a 1–5 agreement rating.","outcome":"The measured response the research question is trying to explain, compare, describe, or predict.","outlier":"An observation unusually far from the rest of the data under a chosen diagnostic rule. An outlier flag is a reason to investigate, not an automatic instruction to delete the value.","paired data":"Two related observations that must stay linked, usually because they come from the same unit measured twice or from deliberately matched units.","parameter":"A quantity describing a population or statistical model, such as a population mean or correlation. A sample estimate is used to learn about it.","p-value":"Under a specified null model and its assumptions, the probability of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the observed statistic. A p-value does not tell us the probability that the null hypothesis is true, or the size or importance of an effect.","population":"The group or system about which the research question is asked. A sample includes some of its units; a census attempts to include every unit in the defined population.","predictor":"A variable used in a model to explain or predict variation in an outcome. It may be observed or assigned. For an observational study, 'measured predictor' or 'exposure' is often clearer than calling the variable a manipulated independent variable.","protocol":"A documented research plan specifying the design, procedures, measurements, data handling and decision rules. Recording the plan before relevant data are examined helps distinguish prespecified choices from later changes, which should be dated and explained.","pseudoreplication":"Treating repeated measurements, subsamples, or clustered observations as if each were an independent treatment replicate when they are not.","qualitative data":"Evidence examined for its meaning, form or context, including interviews, classroom talk, field notes, written responses, diagrams, models and observed interactions. Qualitative analysis may also use counts, but it depends on how the evidence is interpreted.","random assignment":"Using a random process to assign experimental units to conditions. It supports comparisons by reducing systematic differences in assignment, although chance imbalances may remain, especially with few units.","random sampling":"Using a probability process to select units from a defined population or sampling frame. It addresses population representation, which is a different problem from treatment assignment.","reliability":"Consistency or repeatability of measurement under relevant conditions. Reliability does not guarantee that the measure captures the intended construct.","repeated measures":"Multiple measurements from the same unit across times, trials, or conditions. The repeated observations are related and should not usually be treated as independent.","replication":"Independent repetition that provides evidence about variability across independent units. Repeated readings of the same sample are not automatically independent replication.","research question":"The exact question the study will answer with evidence. Its wording should match the design and the claim that can be supported.","sample":"The people, cases, organisms, records or other units selected for study. In a proposal, distinguish a planned or eligible sample from the units that actually contribute evidence.","sampling frame":"The list or operational source from which units can be selected, such as a class roster. Its coverage may differ from the target population, and the method of selection may be probabilistic or non-probabilistic.","sampling method":"The process used to decide which units enter the sample, such as convenience, purposive, simple random, or stratified sampling.","scale":"The form in which a measurement is expressed, including physical units, score ranges, ordered categories, or coding categories.","standard deviation":"A numerical summary of spread around the mean. It is meaningful for numerical data and is abbreviated SD.","statistical significance":"A decision label based on a prespecified statistical rule, often involving a p-value threshold. It does not automatically mean the effect is large, important, causal, or reproducible.","stratification":"Dividing units into meaningful groups before sampling or assignment so those groups are represented or balanced deliberately.","subsample":"A smaller observation taken from within a larger unit, such as several leaves measured from one plant. Subsamples improve information about that unit but do not automatically increase independent replication.","synthesis":"Combining evidence across multiple sources to describe patterns, disagreements, explanations, and boundaries rather than listing each source separately.","technical replicate":"Repeated measurement or processing of the same underlying sample to assess technical variation. It is not the same as an independently treated experimental replicate.","theme":"A meaningful pattern used to organize qualitative evidence or literature synthesis. A theme should be supported by traceable evidence, not merely chosen because it sounds important.","TRAAP":"A source-evaluation checklist covering Timeliness, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, and Purpose. Use the criteria to ask questions about a source and verify its claims rather than treating the checklist as proof of quality.","variable":"A characteristic that can take different values across observations, units, times, or conditions.","within-unit design":"A design in which the same unit contributes data under multiple conditions or times. It creates paired or repeated data.","Welch t-test":"A test for comparing means from two independent groups that does not require equal population variances. Independence and the other assumptions for inference still need to be considered.","ANOVA":"Analysis of variance. A family of methods used to compare mean structure across multiple groups or conditions. The exact ANOVA model must match independence and repeated-measure structure.","McNemar test":"A test comparing marginal proportions for two related binary measurements, usually on the same or matched units. It uses the pairs whose classifications differ and does not, by itself, establish a causal effect or measure agreement.","Cochran Q":"An omnibus test comparing binary outcome proportions across three or more related conditions on the same units. It can indicate whether the proportions differ somewhere across conditions, without identifying which conditions differ.","regression":"A family of statistical models describing relationships between an outcome and one or more predictors. The model must suit the outcome, design and assumptions; a regression coefficient does not automatically represent a causal effect.","citation":"A reference in the text that points to a source. Citation integrity requires the source to exist and actually support the nearby claim.","identifiable data":"Information that directly identifies a person or can reasonably be linked back to them using available information."},"stages":{"1":{"title":"Start with curiosity, then make it researchable","plain":"You are not choosing a statistical test or writing a perfect research question yet. You are collecting possible things worth investigating.","steps":["Write several topics, observations, problems, or claims that genuinely make you curious.","For each idea, ask what evidence could possibly help you learn more about it.","List resources you might use, including materials, organisms, public datasets or observation settings. Check which are accessible and which require permission or confirmation.","Generate at least three directions before choosing one. The goal is to avoid locking onto the first idea just because it appeared first."],"strong":"A strong Stage 1 record gives several possible directions and connects curiosity to a possible source of evidence. It can still be messy.","examples":[{"label":"Biology","text":"Interest: plant stress. Observation: some houseplants near a sunny window appear to wilt sooner. Possible direction: compare soil moisture loss in equally sized containers under defined light conditions, without assuming light alone explains the observation."},{"label":"Learning","text":"Interest: memory. Observation: vocabulary feels easier to remember after self-testing. Possible direction: compare delayed recall after two study procedures."},{"label":"Literature-based","text":"Interest: microplastics. Question I keep seeing: how consistently are microplastics measured across freshwater studies? Possible direction: literature review of sampling and measurement methods."}],"stuck":["List a science topic you would voluntarily watch a video about.","Write one sentence beginning with “I have noticed that…”","Write one claim beginning with “People often say that…” and ask what evidence would be needed to evaluate it.","Choose something you can safely observe or measure with the time and resources you actually have."],"terms":["variable","construct","qualitative data"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student is curious about why some plants in the school courtyard wilt faster.","thinking":["I should not jump straight to 'sun causes wilting.'","I can list several possibilities: light, soil moisture, pot size, watering schedule.","I may be able to measure growing conditions, but I need permission before using school plants or changing their care."],"draft":"Possible direction: observe light exposure and soil moisture over time, or test moisture loss in standardised containers with other conditions controlled."}},"2":{"title":"Shrink the idea until one student project can actually do it","plain":"A topic is still broader than a research question. Here you define the boundaries of the project so it can be completed safely and well.","steps":["Name the broad area, then one narrower subtopic.","Specify what phenomenon, relationship, experience, or problem you care about.","Specify the population, organism, system, setting, or context.","Identify what evidence you could realistically obtain.","Cut away parts that require too much time, unsafe procedures, inaccessible equipment, restricted data, or approvals you cannot obtain."],"strong":"A strong topic is specific enough that you can imagine the evidence you would collect or review, while still leaving room to refine the exact research question.","examples":[{"label":"Too broad → workable","text":"Climate change → urban heat → temperature differences among shaded and unshaded school surfaces during a defined time window."},{"label":"Too broad → workable","text":"Social media → short-form video use → possible association between self-reported evening use and bedtime in a defined student sample, provided participation, consent and school approval are appropriate."}],"stuck":["Add a location or context.","Add a specific population or biological system.","Add one measurable or observable feature.","Ask what part could be completed in the course timeline."],"terms":["population","sample","generalization","observational study"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The student's original topic is 'climate change.'","thinking":["That topic is far too large for one class project.","I can focus on one local phenomenon that is measurable.","Surface temperature in shaded and unshaded school areas is feasible during a defined afternoon period."],"draft":"Working topic: afternoon surface-temperature differences between selected shaded and sun-exposed school surfaces. This local comparison would not establish a climate-change trend."}},"3":{"title":"Read enough background to improve the question before committing","plain":"This is a preliminary scan. You are learning the vocabulary, common methods, and what researchers already know so you do not build a question on a misunderstanding.","steps":["Write the main concepts in your possible topic.","List synonyms, scientific terms, and related phrases researchers might use.","Draft several search strings, then try them in appropriate tools. Record the date, platform and actual terms for each search completed.","Use sources you have actually examined to record supported findings and remaining questions. Mark unverified impressions as possibilities to check.","Notice the methods and measurements researchers actually use.","Revise your possible question if the literature changes your understanding."],"strong":"A strong background scan documents actual sources consulted and sharpens your thinking. It does not pretend to be a complete literature review.","examples":[{"label":"Search vocabulary","text":"Everyday term: studying. Research terms might include retrieval practice, practice testing, rereading, delayed recall, retention."},{"label":"Question shift","text":"One possible revision, pending a review of relevant methods, would move from “Does sleep improve grades?” to “Is self-reported weekday sleep duration associated with current course grade in a defined sample?”"}],"stuck":["Search the broad concept plus “review” to learn field vocabulary.","Look at keywords used by two relevant papers.","Record methods even when the paper does not answer your exact question."],"terms":["construct","operational definition","predictor","outcome"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The student initially searches 'studying better.'","thinking":["The phrase is everyday language, not field vocabulary.","Relevant studies may use terms such as retrieval practice, practice testing, retention and delayed recall. I need to check how each source defines them.","These candidate terms help me plan a more focused search before deciding what the evidence shows."],"draft":"Possible review question to refine after checking relevant sources. How have secondary science education studies defined and measured delayed recall after retrieval practice?"}},"4":{"title":"Write a question that your evidence can actually answer","plain":"The research question is the central contract of the study. It tells you what evidence is needed and limits what the final conclusion can claim.","steps":["Decide whether you mainly want to describe, examine a relationship, test a possible causal effect, understand experiences/processes, or synthesize literature.","Name the key variables/constructs or phenomenon.","Name the population/system/context when it matters.","Use wording that matches the design you can realistically use.","Check that the answer could come from evidence, not opinion.","Write several versions and choose the clearest."],"strong":"A strong question is clear, bounded, answerable, feasible, ethical, and uses causal language only when the planned design can support it.","examples":[{"label":"Descriptive","text":"Within each assigned salinity condition, what proportion of seeds per dish show a visible shoot on Day 7?"},{"label":"Observational","text":"What is the association between reported weekday sleep duration and biology quiz score among students in this class?"},{"label":"Experimental","text":"How does salinity assigned to dishes affect the mean proportion of seeds with a visible shoot per dish on Day 7 under standardised growing conditions?"},{"label":"Qualitative","text":"How do students describe the reasons they hesitate to ask questions during science lessons?"}],"stuck":["Start with “What is…” for description.","Start with “What is the relationship between…” for observational association.","Use “How does assigned/manipulated X affect Y?” only for a genuine intervention design.","Start with “How do participants describe…” for experience-focused qualitative work."],"terms":["research question","association","causal claim","predictor","outcome"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student wants to ask 'Does phone use lower grades?'","thinking":["I will only measure existing phone use, not assign it.","That means the proposed design is observational, with possible confounding and self-report limitations.","My question should ask about association, not whether phone use causes lower grades."],"draft":"What is the association between self-reported daily phone use and current course grade in this sample?"}},"5":{"title":"Build a search that another person could understand and repeat","plain":"A literature search involves choosing concepts, testing appropriate search terms and documenting what you actually did. Keep proposed searches distinct from completed searches.","steps":["Identify two or three concept blocks from the question.","Add relevant synonyms, spelling variants and scientific terms within each block. Check related phrases rather than treating all of them as interchangeable.","Join synonyms with OR and different concepts with AND.","Choose appropriate scholarly search tools, checking access and adapting each search to the platform's syntax.","Set inclusion/exclusion criteria before screening results.","For each search actually run, record the platform, date, exact submitted string, settings or filters, reported results and subsequent changes. Keep proposed queries labelled as plans."],"strong":"A strong search is broad enough to find relevant studies, specific enough to screen efficiently, and documented so your gap claims can be evaluated.","examples":[{"label":"Proposed Boolean structure","text":"(\"retrieval practice\" OR \"practice testing\") AND (biology OR science) AND (retention OR recall)"},{"label":"Candidate concept blocks","text":"Block A: microplastic OR microplastics. Block B: freshwater OR river OR lake. Block C: sampling OR detection OR measurement."}],"stuck":["Copy the main nouns from your research question.","For each noun, ask what another researcher might call the same idea.","Do not add every possible concept with AND; over-constraining a search can hide relevant papers."],"terms":["boolean search","research question","literature gap"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The question concerns retrieval practice in biology learning.","thinking":["My main concepts are retrieval practice, biology/science learning, and delayed retention.","I need synonyms within each concept block.","OR connects synonyms; AND connects different concepts."],"draft":"The following search is proposed and has not yet been run. (\"retrieval practice\" OR \"practice testing\") AND (biology OR science) AND (\"delayed recall\" OR retention). Check the platform syntax and record the actual search when completed."}},"6":{"title":"Turn each source into an evidence record you can use later","plain":"Do not only save a PDF. Extract the information that will let you compare studies and verify later claims.","steps":["Record verified bibliographic information, a stable identifier or link, and page or section locations for important claims.","Record design, sample/context, variables or constructs, measures, and key findings.","Record important limitations.","Use TRAAP questions to examine timeliness, relevance, authority, accuracy and purpose; also assess design and measurement quality directly.","Mark whether the source is included, pending, or excluded and why.","Separate what the authors measured, what they observed, and how they interpreted it."],"strong":"A strong source record makes later synthesis possible while retaining enough source detail to check later claims against the original paper.","examples":[{"label":"Hypothetical extraction","text":"Design: cross-sectional observational. Sample: 420 secondary students. Measure: self-reported activity hours + validated well-being scale. Illustrative finding: a small positive association between reported activity hours and well-being score. Limits: self-report, cross-sectional, possible confounding."}],"stuck":["Start with Methods to find design/sample/measures.","Use Results for the observed evidence.","Use Discussion to record the authors' interpretation and limitations, but do not confuse interpretation with the result itself."],"terms":["TRAAP","observational study","measurement validity","confounder"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A paper reports a strong result but uses one university course and self-reported outcomes.","thinking":["The finding may still be useful, but I need to record the design and measure before citing it broadly.","Peer reviewed does not automatically mean the evidence fits my claim.","I should separate the observed result from the authors' interpretation."],"draft":"Potentially include, subject to the review criteria. Record the limited context and self-reported outcome, and use the source only for claims its methods can support."}},"7":{"title":"Compare studies to find patterns, limits, and a defensible reason for your study","plain":"Synthesis asks what the collection of studies says. You are moving beyond one-paper-at-a-time notes.","steps":["Group studies by themes, methods, populations, measures, or findings.","Identify agreements only where the checked findings and measures support the same comparison.","Identify where results differ and look for design/context explanations.","Track repeated limitations.","Choose a study rationale that the evidence actually supports: replication, extension, inconsistency, method comparison, local question, or a documented gap.","Write a bounded gap/rationale statement."],"strong":"A strong synthesis names both patterns and boundaries and does not claim global novelty from a small search.","examples":[{"label":"Hypothetical synthesis","text":"Fictional illustration only. If three studies actually report delayed-retention outcomes, compare their measures and estimates before describing convergence or differences related to feedback."},{"label":"Hypothetical local rationale","text":"If a documented search finds more university than secondary-school samples among the eligible studies, the review can describe that pattern within its search limits. A small secondary biology classroom project could examine the question locally, without claiming that research with adolescents is absent."}],"stuck":["Put two studies side by side and ask what is the same and what differs.","Compare measures before comparing findings.","If you cannot prove a global gap, use replication, extension, or local relevance as a legitimate rationale."],"terms":["synthesis","theme","literature gap","generalization"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"Four hypothetical studies address the same topic. Three report similar advantages, and the fourth reports a smaller advantage.","thinking":["I should not label the fourth study an outlier and ignore it.","The studies use different feedback timing and retention intervals.","The disagreement may reveal a boundary condition instead of a simple contradiction."],"draft":"Within this fictional example, three studies report a similar advantage and one a smaller one. A real synthesis would need the studies' estimates, designs and retention measures before interpreting that difference."}},"8":{"title":"Write about the literature as a conversation, not a stack of summaries","plain":"A literature review develops an argument from checked sources. Some paragraphs compare several studies; others explain a method, question an interpretation or examine an important source in depth.","steps":["Choose an organizing structure such as themes, methods, chronology, or theory.","Let each paragraph develop one connected idea, with enough explanation to make the evidence and its limits understandable.","Open with the idea, method, finding or source that best advances the argument. Vary sentence structure without sacrificing citation clarity.","Use multiple sources where the claim genuinely depends on multiple studies.","Explain where findings agree or differ when the cited evidence permits comparison, without assuming differences have a known cause.","End the review by showing how the evidence leads to the study purpose/question."],"strong":"A strong review is traceable to sources and demonstrates relationships among studies.","examples":[{"label":"Weak (fictional sources)","text":"Smith studied memory. Lee studied memory. Park studied memory."},{"label":"Stronger (fictional sources)","text":"In this fictional comparison, the three papers use different delayed-recall assessments. Their results would need to be examined in relation to those measures before a review could claim a shared advantage."}],"stuck":["Write the paragraph's idea in one sentence before adding citations.","Ask “What do these sources collectively support?”","If a paragraph contains only one source because the claim truly is source-specific, make that purpose explicit."],"terms":["synthesis","theme","citation","literature gap"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"In a writing exercise with three fictional articles, a student has written one paragraph per source.","thinking":["Readers need a claim about the literature, not a reading log.","I can group the three articles around delayed retention, then compare where they differ.","I should check exactly which sentence each source supports and use the order of ideas that makes the comparison clearest."],"draft":"These fictional articles assess retrieval practice at different delays. The comparison would first establish how each article defines its outcome, then examine any reported differences. A real review must cite the actual studies before making those claims."}},"9":{"title":"Choose a design by asking what kind of answer the question needs","plain":"Research design is the overall evidence structure. Do not choose 'experiment' because it sounds more scientific.","steps":["Identify whether the question asks for description, association, causal effect, experience/process, mixed evidence, or literature synthesis.","Ask whether an exposure is assigned, an intervention occurs without random assignment, or existing patterns are only observed. Different designs require different safeguards.","Ask whether the same unit is measured more than once.","Identify the strongest claim the design could reasonably support.","Choose the design family that matches those answers, including how qualitative and quantitative evidence would be integrated in mixed methods, if relevant."],"strong":"A strong design choice explains why that design can answer the question and explicitly states what it cannot establish.","examples":[{"label":"Association","text":"Question: Is weekly study time associated with exam score? Design: observational correlational, because study time is measured, not assigned."},{"label":"Causal comparison","text":"Question: How does assigned retrieval practice versus rereading affect delayed recall? Design: experimental if units can be appropriately assigned and other validity protections are in place."}],"stuck":["If you do not assign the key exposure, start by thinking observational.","If you mainly want meanings or experiences, consider qualitative.","If all evidence comes from existing studies, consider a literature review or, only when comparable quantitative effects can be pooled appropriately, meta-analysis."],"terms":["observational study","independent variable","exposure","random assignment","causal claim"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student asks whether sleep causes better grades but cannot assign sleep duration.","thinking":["The factor is observed, not manipulated.","A correlational/observational design can estimate association.","Even a strong association would not establish causation."],"draft":"Design: observational correlational. Claim boundary: an association in the participating sample, with the measured variables and possible confounding made explicit."}},"10":{"title":"Translate ideas into exact variables, measurements, units, and comparison roles","plain":"Terms such as stress, memory, growth, engagement, or pollution are ideas until you define exactly how they will appear in the study.","steps":["Decide whether a formal hypothesis is appropriate for this question.","Name each central construct or variable.","Give each variable a role that matches the design.","Write an operational definition: exactly what will be measured, counted, scored, coded, observed, or manipulated.","Identify the experimental or observational unit.","Separate control/comparison conditions from controlled conditions.","List potential alternative explanations or confounders."],"strong":"A strong plan lets another person know exactly what each variable means in this study and what unit provides independent evidence.","examples":[{"label":"Operational definition","text":"Memory retention → number of 20 target definitions recalled correctly 48 hours after study, scored with a prespecified answer key."},{"label":"Unit distinction","text":"If salt solution is assigned to whole dishes, the dish is the experimental unit when conditions are independently assigned to dishes, even if several seeds are measured inside each dish."}],"stuck":["For every abstract word, finish the sentence “In this study, I will know this by…”","Ask what object could independently receive a different treatment.","If you did not manipulate a variable, use predictor/exposure terminology when appropriate."],"terms":["construct","operational definition","experimental unit","confounder","control condition","controlled condition","hypothesis"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student says the independent variable is 'stress' and the dependent variable is 'performance.'","thinking":["Those are still constructs, not exact data.","If stress is observed, calling it a manipulated independent variable is misleading.","I need an operational definition for both constructs and the actual unit."],"draft":"Proposed predictor: score from a specified self-report stress scale. Proposed outcome: number of correct items on the defined task. Observational unit: one participating person. The scale score is a measure of reported stress, not a direct reading of every physiological stress process."}},"11":{"title":"Decide who or what supplies evidence and whether the measurements deserve your interpretation","plain":"Sampling and measurement determine what the data represent. Research ethics, safety, and required permissions also determine whether and how data may be collected.","steps":["Define the target population/system and the actual sample.","Explain how units enter the sample.","State the limits on generalization.","Name the instrument or measurement procedure.","Explain reliability/consistency and why the measure is valid for the intended construct.","Identify privacy, safety, consent, authority, environmental, or other ethical issues.","Identify who must review or approve the plan before recruitment, access to records or data collection begins."],"strong":"A strong plan connects sampling to generalization and measurement evidence to the exact interpretation you want to make.","examples":[{"label":"Sampling","text":"Target population: all students in the school. Planned sample: consenting students from one available biology class, subject to approval. Sampling method: convenience. The final sample and limits on generalisation would be reported after recruitment."},{"label":"Measurement","text":"Illustrative shoot measure: Day 7 length from the substrate surface to the visible shoot tip, recorded in centimetres. A separate status identifies seeds with no visible shoot; consistent measurements do not independently establish germination."}],"stuck":["Do not write 'random' unless a random process is actually used.","Ask who or what you want your conclusion to refer to, then ask who or what you can actually study.","For reliability ask whether the measurement is consistent; for validity ask whether the measurement supports the intended meaning."],"terms":["population","sample","sampling method","random sampling","random assignment","reliability","measurement validity","calibration","blinding"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student has access to one biology class and wants to generalize to all teenagers.","thinking":["Access does not make this a random sample.","The actual sample is one convenience class.","The result can be useful locally, but the generalization has to stay narrow."],"draft":"Planned sampling: invite eligible students in one class through an approved voluntary process. The eventual sample and limits on generalisation depend on who actually participates and contributes analysable data."}},"12":{"title":"Write the protocol and dataset structure before final data collection","plain":"This stage turns the design into an exact procedure and a data table. Decisions made here are easier to defend when they are made before seeing the final results.","steps":["List materials and preparation.","Write ordered procedure steps in enough detail for another person to reproduce the study.","Count independently assigned units separately from subsamples and repeated readings. Retain IDs linking seeds or repeated records to their dish or other unit.","Record randomization, counterbalancing, blinding, standardization, and other protections where applicable.","Define what one dataset row represents.","Define every column, its data type, units/categories, and missing-value code.","Decide missing-data and exclusion rules in advance where possible.","State the planned analysis at the level you can justify before data exist."],"strong":"A strong protocol links each research question component to a measurement and each measurement to a clearly structured dataset.","examples":[{"label":"Row structure","text":"For the illustrative Day 7 salinity protocol, one row records one planted seed, linked to its dish. Columns include dish_id, seed_id, NaCl_mM, visible_shoot and shoot_length_cm, with an observation-status field to distinguish a verified zero from missing data."},{"label":"Binary column","text":"visible_shoot: Binary. On Day 7, 1 means a shoot is visible and 0 means no shoot is visible. An unobserved seed needs a separate missing status. This coding does not establish whether a radicle emerged."}],"stuck":["Imagine handing your procedure and blank data table to another student. What would they still have to guess?","If one unit appears in several rows, include a stable ID.","Use explicit missing codes or documented blank rules; do not let blank mean several different things."],"terms":["protocol","replication","repeated measures","subsample","pseudoreplication","data dictionary","binary","categorical","ordinal","numerical"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student measures 20 seeds in one dish per treatment.","thinking":["Each seed gives a measurement, but the whole dish receives the treatment together.","The dish receives the treatment, so the seeds within it are subsamples rather than independent treatment replicates.","My data table needs dish_id and seed_id so I do not mistake 20 subsamples for 20 independent treatment units."],"draft":"For the proposed salinity protocol, one row records each seed's Day 7 observation with dish_id and seed_id. The analysis must compare independently assigned dishes, not treat seeds sharing a dish as separate treatment replicates."}},"13":{"title":"Protect the raw evidence, then understand what the dataset actually contains","plain":"Before formal statistics, verify data quality, structure, missingness, and patterns. Cleaning decisions must be documented instead of silently changing inconvenient values.","steps":["Preserve an unchanged raw-data file.","Check IDs, units, coding, impossible values, and exact duplicates.","Record missing observations and why they are missing when known.","Document corrections and protocol deviations.","Visualise distributions and the pairing or nesting of observations where applicable; for qualitative evidence, inspect the source corpus and analytic records.","Calculate appropriate descriptive summaries.","Keep the study's independent/repeated structure visible."],"strong":"A strong Stage 13 record can explain where the raw file is, what was changed in any cleaned file, what is missing, and what the data look like before inference.","examples":[{"label":"Numerical","text":"For the illustrative salinity study, shoot_length_cm is numerical. Inspect its distribution and the per-dish summaries, with means/SDs or medians/IQRs where useful. Preserve dish membership."},{"label":"Categorical/binary","text":"The Day 7 visible_shoot field is binary when observed (1 = visible, 0 = not visible). Summarise recorded values with counts and proportions, keeping unobserved seeds distinct from observed zeroes. It does not directly measure radicle emergence."}],"stuck":["Start by asking what one row represents.","Check each column against the data dictionary.","Plot before testing.","Do not delete an unusual value until you investigate whether it is an error, a real extreme observation, or a protocol deviation."],"terms":["binary","categorical","numerical","ordinal","missing data","outlier","descriptive statistics","standard deviation","interquartile range","paired data"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"One plant height is much larger than the rest.","thinking":["An unusual value is not automatically an error.","First I check the source record and measurement notes.","If valid, I retain it and document sensitivity/diagnostic concerns instead of deleting it to improve results."],"draft":"Hypothetical data-check note. One unusually large measurement was checked against the original record and retained. The analysis will show whether conclusions are sensitive to it."}},"14":{"title":"Choose analysis from the question, design, unit structure, and outcome type","plain":"Begin with the research question and the structure of the evidence. Quantitative studies specify an estimate of interest; qualitative studies identify the phenomenon and interpretive question.","steps":["For quantitative work, state the estimand in ordinary language, such as a mean difference, paired change, proportion, association, or slope. For qualitative work, identify the phenomenon and interpretive question instead.","Identify the outcome type: numerical, count, ordinal, binary, nominal categorical, or text.","Identify whether groups are independent or the same units are repeated/paired.","Check whether observations are nested or clustered.","Choose a descriptive or inferential method that matches that structure.","Check relevant assumptions using plots and design information.","Plan to report magnitude and uncertainty, not only a p-value."],"strong":"A strong analysis plan can explain why the method answers the research question and why the data satisfy the structure the method expects.","examples":[{"label":"Binary","text":"Correct/incorrect measured before and after on the same students → paired binary data. A McNemar-type analysis preserves the pairing."},{"label":"Two independent numerical groups","text":"Plants independently assigned to fertiliser A or B, with a numerical growth outcome → compare group distributions and means; Welch's t-test can be appropriate when the mean difference answers the question and its other assumptions are met."},{"label":"Repeated binary 3+","text":"The same units measured yes/no under three conditions → repeated binary structure; Cochran's Q is one possible omnibus analysis."}],"stuck":["Ask 'same units or different units?'","Ask 'numbers, categories, ordered categories, two-category outcome, or text?'","Ask 'what exact difference/association/proportion do I want to estimate?'","Use the wizard only after answering those questions."],"terms":["estimand","numerical","categorical","binary","ordinal","nominal","paired data","independent groups","effect size","confidence interval","p-value","Welch t-test","ANOVA","McNemar test","Cochran Q"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The same students are coded correct/incorrect before and after an activity.","thinking":["The outcome has exactly two categories, so it is binary.","The same students appear twice, so the data are paired.","I need a paired binary analysis, not a test that treats the two proportions as independent groups."],"draft":"Structure: paired binary. Estimand: change in the proportion correct based on within-student category changes; consider McNemar-type analysis."}},"15":{"title":"Report what the evidence shows and how the findings were established","plain":"Results should show what the evidence supports. When Results and Discussion are separate, broader explanations and implications generally belong in Discussion; qualitative Findings may also include interpretation grounded in the data.","steps":["State the analyzed sample or corpus and any exclusions.","Report descriptive evidence first when useful.","Report the primary estimate or pattern.","Give uncertainty, test statistic, p-value, and effect size when appropriate to the method.","Report secondary and null/weak/unexpected results honestly.","Point readers to the exact table/figure/output supporting each statement.","Do not upgrade association to causation because a p-value is small."],"strong":"A strong Results section is traceable, complete, neutral, and includes weak or contradictory findings.","examples":[{"label":"Quantitative style","text":"Illustrative reporting structure only. State the observed mean difference in centimetres, the calculated confidence interval and the corresponding test results when the chosen analysis supports them. Do not insert invented values."},{"label":"Boundary","text":"The variables were positively associated. This wording reports the observed relationship without claiming one caused the other."}],"stuck":["Write what happened numerically or thematically before writing why.","When explaining a finding, check whether your interpretation is supported directly by the evidence or belongs in a broader Discussion.","For every number ask where it came from."],"terms":["effect size","confidence interval","p-value","statistical significance","inference"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"In a hypothetical observational study, self-reported sleep duration and test scores are positively associated (r = .52, p < .01).","thinking":["The illustrative coefficient and p-value are insufficient as a full report. I need the analysable sample size, how variables were measured and an uncertainty estimate where available.","The small p-value does not turn the study into an experiment.","I should not explain the mechanism in Results."],"draft":"Hypothetical example only. Reported sleep duration and test scores were positively associated (r = .52, p < .01); the actual Results section would also state the sample size and appropriate uncertainty, with values taken from verified output."}},"16":{"title":"Explain what the results mean while keeping every claim inside the study's limits","plain":"Discussion develops an evidence-grounded answer to the research question. The relationship between findings, possible explanations, earlier research and limitations should determine how the argument unfolds.","steps":["Give a direct answer to the research question.","Identify the key findings that most directly answer the research question.","For each finding, link the claim to exact evidence.","Consider plausible interpretations, making clear which processes were directly measured and which remain proposed explanations.","Consider alternative explanations.","Compare with relevant literature.","Explain how material limitations affect particular interpretations, rather than adding a routine list of weaknesses.","State realistic implications and specific future research."],"strong":"A strong Discussion makes reasoning visible and distinguishes what the study observed from what the author thinks may explain it.","examples":[{"label":"Correlational boundary","text":"The positive association is consistent with the proposed explanation, but unmeasured study habits and reverse direction remain plausible, so the result does not establish causation."},{"label":"Experimental boundary","text":"The treatment difference occurred under these laboratory conditions; limited replication and one organism type restrict broader generalization."}],"stuck":["For each key finding, ask which evidence supports the interpretation, which alternative explanations remain, and what the study cannot establish.","For a limitation, identify the affected finding, then explain what additional evidence would be needed for a stronger interpretation.","Compare studies on design and measurement, not only whether the direction matched."],"terms":["causal claim","confounder","generalization","external validity","attrition"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The hypothetical observational study from the previous stage shows a positive sleep-score association.","thinking":["My direct answer can say the variables were associated.","Possible explanations include sleep affecting learning, study routines affecting both, or reverse direction.","I need to explain how self-report and convenience sampling limit the claim."],"draft":"In this hypothetical sample, reported sleep duration and test scores were positively associated. The self-reported measure and observational design leave alternative explanations open, while the local sample limits claims about other students."}},"17":{"title":"Close the argument carefully, then write an abstract that reflects the work","plain":"The conclusion brings the work back to its research question without exceeding the evidence. The abstract summarises the whole paper and is usually easiest to write after the main text is stable.","steps":["Write a conclusion that answers the question at the level the design supports.","Include the central evidence without introducing a new analysis.","State an important boundary or limitation when needed.","Write an abstract suited to the type of work. For a completed empirical study, include the purpose, design and sample or system, principal findings, and bounded conclusion. For a proposal, describe the planned inquiry without inventing results.","Write a specific title that identifies the actual study or proposed inquiry without implying a result or causal effect not established.","Choose keywords a reader might use to find the study."],"strong":"A strong abstract accurately summarises the work, its approach, and findings when available, with conclusions limited to what the evidence supports.","examples":[{"label":"Weak abstract ending","text":"The implications are discussed."},{"label":"Stronger function","text":"For a completed study, summarise the primary observed finding and the conclusion it supports. For a proposal, describe the planned approach without presenting anticipated results as findings."}],"stuck":["Imagine a reader sees only the abstract. Can they tell what was completed or proposed, and which findings are actually available?","Remove claims broader than your design or sample.","Make the title name the phenomenon/variables and useful context."],"terms":["generalization","causal claim","effect size","confidence interval"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"In a hypothetical completed study, the abstract includes background and method but omits the main finding.","thinking":["A reader should know what the study actually observed.","I can summarize the main estimate/pattern without reproducing calculations.","The conclusion must stay within the design."],"draft":"A completed-study abstract would state the verified primary finding and then explain the scope of its interpretation. The wording depends on the actual outcome and design."}},"18":{"title":"Audit the paper as one evidence chain","plain":"Final proofreading is more than grammar. Every major claim should connect backward to evidence and every citation should support the nearby statement.","steps":["Trace the research question into the method. For completed work, was suitable evidence collected or reviewed? For planned work, is the proposed approach capable of addressing the question?","Trace the method into the evidence record. Are observations, documents, transcripts, or data units defined and traceable?","Trace evidence into analysis. Quantitative methods must respect unit structure and outcome type; qualitative and review approaches should document how evidence was selected and interpreted.","Trace analysis into findings. Can each reported number, theme, or substantive evidence claim be traced to its source?","Trace Results into Discussion and Conclusion: do claims stay within design and generalization limits?","Verify citations against the actual sources. For recoverable sources, check the corresponding reference entries and apply the required style's exceptions.","Read the full document for accurate claims, natural connections between adjacent sentences and paragraphs, repeated rhetorical patterns, Canadian English, and consistency with related sections."],"strong":"A strong final paper lets readers trace its conclusions to the evidence and its citations, while the prose develops as a connected, varied argument.","examples":[{"label":"Alignment problem","text":"Question asks whether X causes Y, but X was only observed. Fixing grammar cannot repair this; the causal wording must be revised."},{"label":"Citation problem","text":"A citation is present, but the cited study used a different population and does not support the universal claim. Narrow the claim or use appropriate evidence."}],"stuck":["Read only the research question and conclusion. Do they match?","Read only the Method and Results. Are all reported analyses based on planned/recorded data?","Open each source for any claim that feels stronger than the evidence."],"terms":["citation","causal claim","generalization","protocol","p-value","effect size"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The conclusion says 'X caused Y,' but the Method describes an observational study.","thinking":["This is an alignment problem, not a grammar problem.","The research question, Results, Discussion, and title all need the same claim ceiling.","I should trace the wording backward through the design before final submission."],"draft":"Revise causal language to association language throughout the paper unless the design genuinely supports causal inference."}}},"fields":{"interest1":{"meaning":"Three science or research areas that genuinely interest you. Each can begin with a topic, observation, problem, or claim.","write":"List three areas and, if helpful, add a curiosity within each. None needs to be a research question yet.","example":"Leaf variation in sun and shade; learning vocabulary; water quality in streams.","questions":["Would I still care about this after spending several weeks on it?","Can I imagine any evidence that might help me learn about it?"],"avoid":"Do not write a topic just because it sounds academically impressive."},"problem1":{"meaning":"A specific situation within your interest that seems worth explaining, describing, comparing, or improving.","write":"Describe the observable problem or uncertainty in one or two sentences.","example":"Hypothetical observation: in one class, some students recalled vocabulary on quiz day but struggled to retrieve it a week later.","questions":["What exactly is happening?","Who or what is affected?","What part is uncertain?"],"avoid":"Avoid writing only a broad social or scientific issue without a specific researchable problem."},"claim1":{"meaning":"A statement people make that could be examined with evidence.","write":"Write the claim as someone might say it, then keep an open mind about whether the evidence will support it.","example":"Using music while studying improves memory.","questions":["What evidence would make me more or less confident in this claim?","Does the claim imply causation?"],"avoid":"Do not treat the claim as true before investigating it."},"access1":{"meaning":"Evidence sources, tools, settings or materials that may be available for a project, including any permissions or access arrangements still needed.","write":"List resources you can use and distinguish confirmed access from what still requires approval, equipment or a licence.","example":"Possible sources include greenhouse plants, a classroom thermometer, research databases and public weather records. Greenhouse access and equipment use still require confirmation.","questions":["Which resources are already available, and which remain conditional on permission?","Could you obtain the needed evidence during the project timeline?"],"avoid":"Do not assume that a resource is authorised for research merely because you can see or access it."},"ideaA":{"meaning":"Your first possible research direction.","write":"Write a short direction that connects an interest to a possible kind of evidence.","example":"Compare water loss from identical soil containers placed under two light conditions.","questions":["What would I observe or measure?","Is this safe and feasible?"],"avoid":"Do not spend time making this wording perfect yet."},"ideaB":{"meaning":"A second possible research direction that is meaningfully different from Idea A.","write":"Generate another route, preferably using a different question, context, or type of evidence.","example":"Review published studies comparing retrieval practice and rereading in science learning.","questions":["Does this give me a real alternative?","Would this route fit my time/resources better?"],"avoid":"Avoid rewriting Idea A with only one word changed."},"ideaC":{"meaning":"A third possible research direction used to prevent premature commitment.","write":"Write another feasible option before you decide which project to pursue.","example":"Observe whether shaded and unshaded school surfaces differ in afternoon temperature.","questions":["What new evidence source does this idea use?","Would I enjoy investigating this more than A or B?"],"avoid":"Do not skip this simply because your first idea seems good."},"broadTopic":{"meaning":"The large subject area you are interested in. It should still be broader than the final project.","write":"Name a field-sized area, usually a short phrase.","example":"Plant stress; memory and learning; freshwater pollution.","questions":["What general area do I keep returning to?","Could many different research questions fit under this phrase?"],"avoid":"Do not try to make this the final research question."},"subtopic":{"meaning":"A narrower part of the broad topic that can become one project.","write":"Name the specific process, relationship, population, mechanism, or context you want to focus on.","example":"Broad topic: plant stress. Subtopic: early seedling responses to salinity.","questions":["What part of the broad topic can one student study well?","What am I deliberately leaving out?"],"avoid":"Avoid using the same wording as the broad topic."},"phenomenon":{"meaning":"The specific pattern, relationship, experience, process, or problem you want to understand.","write":"Describe what seems to happen, vary, or need explanation.","example":"Possible phenomenon to investigate: variation in early seedling growth across assigned salinity conditions.","questions":["What exactly changes or varies?","What is the observable pattern?"],"avoid":"Avoid writing only a subject name such as “plants.”"},"contextPop":{"meaning":"Who, what, or where the possible study concerns.","write":"Name the population, organism, system, setting, and useful boundaries.","example":"Radish seeds planted in labelled classroom dishes for a proposed seven-day salinity investigation.","questions":["Who or what would supply the evidence?","Where and when does the question apply?"],"avoid":"Do not use a population broader than you can justify."},"scope":{"meaning":"The part of the larger problem that this one project can reasonably address.","write":"State what you will focus on and what you will leave outside the project.","example":"Focus on early seedling growth over 7 days, not long-term plant fitness.","questions":["What can I complete well in the available time?","What tempting extra question should I deliberately leave out?"],"avoid":"Do not promise to solve the entire field."},"timeLimit":{"meaning":"The time available for planning, permissions, collecting or retrieving evidence, analysing records and writing.","write":"State the calendar or number of class periods/weeks you truly have.","example":"For an illustrative six-week project, about eight class periods could be reserved for collecting evidence, subject to the actual course calendar.","questions":["Does my proposed measurement need longer than this?","Do I need time for pilot testing or repeated observations?"],"avoid":"Do not plan a long-term process that cannot occur during the course timeline."},"resources":{"meaning":"The equipment, software, databases, materials, expertise, or permissions the project needs.","write":"List what is required and note anything you do not yet have.","example":"For the proposed salinity study, 18 labelled dishes, 90 radish seeds, a ruler, prepared solutions and a data sheet would be needed. Supervision and laboratory access still require confirmation.","questions":["Which resource would prevent the study if it were unavailable?","What lower-cost, safer or evidence-based alternative could work?"],"avoid":"Do not list resources you only hope will become available later as if they are guaranteed."},"safety":{"meaning":"Any physical, biological, chemical, privacy, participant, environmental, or authority issue that could make the project inappropriate without review.","write":"Name possible risks early, even if you think they can be controlled.","example":"Human caffeine assignment would create health/authority concerns; use a safer observational or literature-based route instead.","questions":["Could anyone be harmed, pressured, identified, or exposed?","Does the method involve organisms, chemicals, or procedures needing supervision?"],"avoid":"Do not wait until the final method to think about safety."},"topicChoice":{"meaning":"The research direction you are currently choosing to develop.","write":"State the chosen topic as a specific phrase and briefly keep its boundaries in mind.","example":"Early seedling growth under different salinity conditions.","questions":["Why is this more feasible or meaningful than the other ideas?","Can I already imagine a question and evidence source?"],"avoid":"Do not confuse the chosen topic with the final research question."},"concepts":{"meaning":"The main ideas in your possible research question that will become search concepts.","write":"List the two to four central concepts separately.","example":"retrieval practice; biology learning; delayed recall","questions":["Which words in my possible question carry the main meaning?","Which concept could have several research synonyms?"],"avoid":"Do not copy every word from the question, including unimportant connecting words."},"synonyms":{"meaning":"Synonyms and related search terms used in the literature. Note when related terms represent different constructs.","write":"Build a small synonym bank for each concept.","example":"retrieval practice: practice testing, test-enhanced learning; delayed recall: retention after a delay; related search term: delayed test","questions":["What term would a journal article use?","Are there older/newer names for this idea?"],"avoid":"Do not join words that are related but mean different constructs as if they were true synonyms."},"prelimSearch":{"meaning":"Proposed searches and records of any preliminary searches actually run while learning the field, before finalising a formal search strategy.","write":"Record planned queries and intended platforms. For each search actually run, add its exact wording, platform, date, relevant results and what you learned or changed.","example":"Proposed Google Scholar query, 'retrieval practice biology retention'. If run, record the actual date, returned sources and useful terms before reporting any findings.","questions":["Which query and platform would you try next, or which did you actually use?","What did a completed search reveal about concepts, methods or the question?"],"avoid":"Do not present a proposed query as a completed search or use a quick scan to assert that a research gap exists."},"known":{"meaning":"A pattern in the research literature supported by sources you have checked, or a provisional claim that still needs source verification.","write":"Summarise a recurring finding only after examining the relevant sources and their limits. Until then, label the claim as something to investigate.","example":"Claim to investigate. Retrieval practice may support delayed recall under some learning conditions; the applicable participants, measures and source evidence still need checking.","questions":["Which studies have you actually read that support the claim?","Are the populations, methods and outcomes sufficiently comparable?"],"avoid":"Do not describe an unverified possibility as established knowledge or make a universal claim from one source."},"methodsSeen":{"meaning":"Methods and measures identified in sources you have read, or categories you intend to extract when those sources become available.","write":"For checked sources, name the design, instruments, outcomes and timing with source identifiers. For a proposed review, list the corresponding extraction categories without calling them observed patterns.","example":"A proposed extraction table could record whether study conditions were assigned, how delayed recall was measured and the interval before assessment. Confirm these details from each source.","questions":["Which designs or measures are documented in the papers you examined?","Which additional information should you extract before comparing their methods?"],"avoid":"Do not call a method common before checking the literature, or copy it without assessing whether it fits your question."},"uncertain":{"meaning":"An unresolved comparison, inconsistent finding or unanswered question, identified in checked literature or proposed for further investigation.","write":"Describe what remains uncertain and whether the uncertainty is based on source evidence already read or is a comparison you still need to examine.","example":"If retrieved studies measure recall at different intervals, assess whether their outcomes can be compared meaningfully before claiming that their findings conflict.","questions":["Which specific feature or finding needs clarification?","Is the uncertainty documented in checked sources or still a question to investigate?"],"avoid":"Do not treat an unverified difference or every reported limitation as a demonstrated research gap."},"questionShift":{"meaning":"How your question changed after learning field vocabulary, methods, or limitations.","write":"Write the earlier idea and the improved direction, then explain what caused the change.","example":"In a hypothetical reading exercise, 'Does sleep improve grades?' becomes 'How is reported weekday sleep duration associated with biology quiz scores?' because the planned design records sleep rather than assigning it.","questions":["Which checked source, research term or methodological insight prompted the revision?","Did the revised question change the outcome, evidence or claim it could support?"],"avoid":"Do not pretend your question never changed; revision is part of research."},"rq1":{"meaning":"Your first complete research-question candidate.","write":"Write one answerable question using the best current wording.","example":"What is the association between weekly study time and delayed biology recall in this class?","questions":["What evidence would answer this?","Does it imply more than the design can support?"],"avoid":"Do not worry if this is not the final version."},"rq2":{"meaning":"A second research-question candidate that changes the evidence, design, scope or purpose in a meaningful way.","write":"Develop another answerable question, explaining how its evidence or focus differs from the first candidate.","example":"How do students in this class describe the study practices they use when preparing for delayed biology recall tasks?","questions":["What evidence would this alternative need?","Does the new question offer a meaningful route distinct from the first?"],"avoid":"Do not produce a second candidate by changing punctuation or a few words while keeping the same inquiry."},"rq3":{"meaning":"A third research-question candidate that offers a different, defensible route through the evidence.","write":"Try another question that changes the source of evidence, inquiry type or focus, then compare its feasibility with the first two.","example":"How have published secondary science studies defined and measured delayed recall following retrieval practice?","questions":["What evidence would this third question require that the first two would not?","Which candidate is most defensible given the actual sources and permissions?"],"avoid":"Do not add another version that merely repeats a previous question in different words."},"questionType":{"meaning":"The primary kind of answer your current research question seeks, chosen from the available categories.","write":"Select the matching question family from the available menu. Explain separately whether the actual design can support the kind of inference the question asks for.","example":"For a question about measured sleep duration and quiz scores, choose 'Correlational / observational'.","questions":["Is the question descriptive, observational, experimental, qualitative, review-based or mixed in purpose?","Does the selected question family match the evidence that can realistically be collected?"],"avoid":"Do not select a causal or experimental question solely because you hope to show an effect."},"finalRQ":{"meaning":"The exact question your project is designed to answer with evidence.","write":"Write one clear sentence that names the key phenomenon/variables and useful population/context. Use wording compatible with the design.","example":"What is the association between reported weekday sleep duration and biology quiz score among students in this class?","questions":["Could the planned evidence answer every part of this sentence?","Does the wording imply causation that my design cannot support?"],"avoid":"Avoid vague questions such as “How does sleep affect students?”"},"rqJustification":{"meaning":"Why the question can be answered responsibly within this project.","write":"Explain feasibility, access to evidence, ethics/safety, and why the question is scientifically or locally useful.","example":"Students could report their sleep duration voluntarily. Linking their responses to quiz records would require consent, appropriate permission and coded matching, so the linked dataset would not be anonymous. The planned study can examine an association within this group.","questions":["Which access or approvals remain unconfirmed?","Can the proposed evidence be obtained safely within the available time?","What would the design allow you to conclude?"],"avoid":"Do not use “interesting” as the only justification."},"searchBlock1":{"meaning":"One major concept from the research question plus alternative words researchers may use.","write":"List the main term and several true synonyms or closely related research terms.","example":"retrieval practice, practice testing, self-testing","questions":["What else might authors call this idea?","Are there spelling or terminology differences?"],"avoid":"Do not mix unrelated concepts in the same OR block."},"searchBlock2":{"meaning":"The second search-concept group, here concerned with the delayed-learning outcome rather than the retrieval activity.","write":"Collect terms for delayed recall or retention and check whether related labels refer to the same outcome, a wider concept or a measurement procedure.","example":"'delayed recall' OR 'delayed retention' OR 'retention test', with the last term checked as a possible assessment method rather than a synonym.","questions":["How does this outcome concept differ from Block 1?","Could a broader term retrieve studies measuring a different construct?"],"avoid":"Do not assume that a test name and the learning outcome it assesses are interchangeable."},"searchBlock3":{"meaning":"An optional population or context block, used only when those limits are relevant and will not hide important studies.","write":"Add population or setting terms when needed, and check whether they exclude relevant evidence.","example":"adolescent* OR 'secondary school' OR 'high school'","questions":["Does this additional block improve the search without excluding relevant records?","Are school-setting terms always present in searchable titles or abstracts?"],"avoid":"Do not force a third block into every search."},"searchStrings":{"meaning":"The exact Boolean queries proposed for each database, followed by the verbatim queries recorded once searches are performed.","write":"Write complete database-specific strings with concept groupings, quotation marks and any necessary syntax or field tags. Keep proposals separate from executed search logs.","example":"Proposed ERIC query, ('retrieval practice' OR 'practice testing') AND ('delayed recall' OR 'retention test'). Add ('secondary school' OR adolescent*) only after checking its effect on retrieval.","questions":["Does each query combine the concepts that appear in its research question?","Which database-specific syntax or population restrictions still need testing?"],"avoid":"Do not report a planned query as executed or substitute an ungrouped keyword list for the query actually submitted."},"databases":{"meaning":"Scholarly databases or other search systems selected for the topic, with their access status and intended coverage.","write":"Name intended databases and explain their complementary coverage. Verify which platforms you can access and separately record which were actually searched.","example":"ERIC and Education Source are planned for education research; Scopus could broaden disciplinary coverage if access is available.","questions":["Which sources are relevant to this evidence base?","Is access confirmed, and can you document the search on each platform?"],"avoid":"Do not imply that naming a database means its coverage was searched or that access is guaranteed."},"includeCriteria":{"meaning":"Source characteristics that must be met for inclusion in the planned evidence set.","write":"Specify population, setting, phenomenon or intervention, outcome and source type where relevant. Decide eligibility without using whether the result supports your expectation.","example":"Include original empirical studies involving secondary-school science learners, a retrieval-based activity and a later learning outcome, regardless of the direction of the reported finding.","questions":["Would the rule include a relevant study with an unexpected or null result?","Can a reader apply the criterion before judging its conclusions?"],"avoid":"Do not widen the eligible population to include university studies when the question is limited to secondary-school science without documenting that decision."},"excludeCriteria":{"meaning":"Explicit, reproducible reasons for excluding sources that do not meet the planned evidence requirements.","write":"State exclusions that mirror inclusion rules, with source types and missing required measures specified where relevant. Record actual reasons at the relevant screening stage.","example":"Exclude studies outside secondary-school science, reports without an identifiable retrieval activity or later learning outcome, and non-empirical commentaries when the review requires original empirical evidence.","questions":["Do these exclusions follow from the inclusion rules?","Could the same rule be applied regardless of whether the result is favourable?"],"avoid":"Do not exclude studies because their findings challenge the proposed explanation."},"searchLog":{"meaning":"A record of searches actually executed, with any earlier search plans retained separately.","write":"For each run, record the database, platform, actual date, exact submitted query, fields, filters and retrieved count. Add screening outcomes later and document query revisions.","example":"Blank log template, not an executed search. [actual date] | [database and platform] | [query as run] | [filters] | [retrieved count] | [screening status] | [reason for revision].","questions":["Could someone reproduce the query using the recorded database, platform and filters?","Are planned strings kept separate from the dates and counts observed in real searches?"],"avoid":"Do not invent dates, retrieval counts or screening results, or keep only the most successful query."},"theme1":{"meaning":"A cross-study pattern or idea supported by more than one source when possible.","write":"State the pattern, then identify which sources support, complicate, or contradict it.","example":"If supported by the included studies, a theme might describe retrieval-based practice as associated with stronger delayed retention across the tasks examined.","questions":["Which sources support this?","Which source does not fit cleanly?"],"avoid":"Do not make a theme that is simply one article title."},"theme2":{"meaning":"A second cross-study pattern that helps organize the literature.","write":"State a pattern distinct from Theme 1 and link it to source evidence.","example":"Possible second theme, if supported by the sources: feedback timing may help explain differences in the reported retrieval-practice advantage.","questions":["Which studies contribute?","Does this overlap too much with Theme 1?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a theme before checking whether multiple studies support it."},"theme3":{"meaning":"An optional third cross-study pattern or methodological theme.","write":"Use it when the evidence base genuinely contains another useful pattern.","example":"If the included studies use different outcome measures, explain how those differences may limit comparisons of reported effects.","questions":["Is this a finding theme or a methods theme?","Does it help explain disagreement?"],"avoid":"Do not add themes merely to reach a target number."},"tensions":{"meaning":"Important differences, contradictions, or conditions that make the literature less simple.","write":"Describe what differs and what methodological/context feature may explain it.","example":"If some studies report smaller advantages on immediate tests, compare the test intervals and designs before interpreting the difference as a boundary condition.","questions":["Do the studies use the same measure?","Do populations or designs differ?"],"avoid":"Do not treat disagreement as proof that one paper is wrong."},"repeatedLimits":{"meaning":"Limitations that recur across several studies and therefore shape what the literature can establish.","write":"Name repeated limitations and identify the studies in which they occur.","example":"If several included studies use short retention intervals or convenience university samples, identify those studies and explain how each limitation affects the synthesis.","questions":["Does this limitation actually recur?","How does it restrict the collective evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not copy each paper's limitation list without identifying patterns."},"gapType":{"meaning":"The most accurate kind of justification for doing your study.","write":"Choose the smallest defensible rationale. A project can be valuable without proving that no research exists.","example":"If prior work exists but uses university samples, choose extension to a new context/population rather than documented literature gap.","questions":["Am I repeating, extending, comparing methods, addressing inconsistency, or asking a local practical question?"],"avoid":"Do not choose 'documented literature gap' simply because it sounds strongest."},"gapStatement":{"meaning":"A precise statement of what your project will add, based on the evidence you actually reviewed.","write":"State a bounded gap, replication, extension, inconsistency, method comparison, or local question.","example":"If few studies in the documented search involve secondary biology classrooms, this project could be described as a local extension. Identify the studies that support that assessment.","questions":["What source evidence supports this statement?","Would 'extension' be more accurate than 'no research exists'?"],"avoid":"Avoid global absence claims unless the search genuinely supports them."},"litToRQ":{"meaning":"The logical bridge from the literature synthesis to your exact research question.","write":"Explain how the patterns, limits, or rationale lead to the question you will ask.","example":"If the reviewed evidence suggests a retrieval benefit under specific conditions and leaves the local classroom context uncertain, the project could ask whether a similar pattern appears in that setting.","questions":["What literature evidence makes this question reasonable?","Is the question still broader than the reviewed evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not introduce a research question unrelated to the literature you just reviewed."},"litIntroPlan":{"meaning":"The opening work of a literature review, including its scope, necessary definitions and rationale for examining the sources.","write":"Introduce the inquiry and relevant terms, identify the evidence boundary and preview only the comparisons the verified literature can sustain.","example":"Define retrieval practice and delayed recall, delimit the relevant science-learning settings, then introduce the source-based comparisons that the reviewed papers support.","questions":["What must a reader understand before evaluating the studies?","Which proposed themes still need source verification?"],"avoid":"Do not begin with an article-by-article list or announce a research gap before demonstrating its basis."},"litThemePlan":{"meaning":"The order and purpose of evidence-based sections or paragraphs in the literature synthesis.","write":"Organise the review around meaningful comparisons, with the necessary source IDs and qualifications. Use as many sections as the actual evidence needs.","example":"Possible sections, subject to the sources reviewed, could compare delayed-recall measures and the conditions under which feedback was provided. Add another section only if it develops a distinct supported point.","questions":["Which sources support the comparison in each section?","Does each section develop the argument in a way the previous section has prepared?"],"avoid":"Do not impose a fixed number of themes or organise solely by author when an evidence-based comparison would be clearer."},"litTensionPlan":{"meaning":"Where the review needs to examine documented disagreement, mixed evidence or limits on comparison.","write":"Identify the conflicting or difficult-to-compare findings and place them near the claim they qualify. Explain which methodological differences may matter without assuming they caused the divergence.","example":"If two verified studies report different retrieval-practice estimates, compare their feedback schedules, assessment intervals and outcome definitions before interpreting the difference.","questions":["Which findings actually differ, and how do you know?","Do the study designs permit a direct comparison or only a cautious explanation?"],"avoid":"Do not conceal inconvenient evidence or turn a possible methodological explanation into a demonstrated cause."},"litEndPlan":{"meaning":"How the literature review's final passage weighs the evidence and prepares the reader for the present question.","write":"Conclude with the source-supported pattern or unresolved comparison, explain its boundaries and show why the chosen question follows.","example":"Close with the findings the checked sources genuinely support, the relevant measurement or context limits, and the rationale for the proposed secondary biology inquiry.","questions":["Does the closing claim remain within the evidence actually examined?","Can the reader see why the research question follows from the synthesis?"],"avoid":"Do not introduce an unverified consistent pattern, a new theme or an unsupported claim of novelty at the end."},"litDraft":{"meaning":"Working literature-review prose that develops claims through source-based comparison, explanation and qualification.","write":"Build connected paragraphs from verified source notes. Check every statement against its citation, and let the nature of the evidence determine each paragraph's development.","example":"Fictional drafting illustration only. Studies A and B used different delayed-recall measures, which would restrict direct comparison of their outcomes. Replace these placeholders with checked studies and citations before making the claim.","questions":["Which checked studies support the point developed across these sentences?","How does the next sentence deepen, qualify or test the preceding claim?"],"avoid":"Do not present fictional sources as genuine findings or rely on a sequence of isolated author summaries when the question needs synthesis."},"designType":{"meaning":"The overall structure used to obtain evidence.","write":"Choose the design family that matches the research question and how the key variables will be generated.","example":"For a question about measured sleep and quiz scores without assigned sleep conditions, select 'Correlational / observational'.","questions":["Is the explanatory factor assigned, or is it recorded without assignment?","Does the question seek a numerical pattern, an account of experiences, or a synthesis of research?"],"avoid":"Do not label a study experimental just because it has groups."},"designWhy":{"meaning":"Your reasoning for why this design can answer the question.","write":"Connect the design directly to the wording of the research question.","example":"Because sleep is measured rather than assigned, an observational correlational design can estimate association but cannot establish a causal sleep effect.","questions":["What evidence will this design generate?","What claim will it not support?"],"avoid":"Do not write only “because it is easiest.”"},"comparisonStructure":{"meaning":"Exactly which groups, conditions, times, or variables are compared, or what is simply described.","write":"Specify the observed or assigned units, the comparison conditions or time points, and whether records are independent, paired or nested. For qualitative work, identify the cases and evidence being compared or described.","example":"The same students measured before and after a lesson form pairs. In the proposed salinity experiment, 18 dishes are independently assigned across three NaCl concentrations, with five seed records nested in each dish.","questions":["Which underlying units contribute the records?","Do comparisons use independent units, linked observations or different cases?"],"avoid":"Do not count repeated seed measurements, participant responses or documents from one underlying unit as independent without examining their dependence."},"claimBoundary":{"meaning":"The strongest conclusion the design could support and an important conclusion it cannot support.","write":"Write both the allowed and disallowed claim.","example":"Can estimate association in this sample; cannot establish that phone use causes lower scores.","questions":["Which important conclusion would the design still not establish even if the observed pattern were clear?","What does the sample permit you to say about other settings?"],"avoid":"Do not let the p-value determine the causal claim."},"hypothesisNeeded":{"meaning":"Whether a formal prediction is appropriate for this study.","write":"Choose yes only when the study is genuinely testing a prespecified prediction that fits the design.","example":"Descriptive survey of species frequencies → a formal directional hypothesis may not be needed.","questions":["Does the study actually test a prespecified prediction, or does it explore a descriptive or interpretive question?","Would a formal hypothesis guide the analysis or merely add an unsupported requirement?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a hypothesis because you think every paper must have one."},"researchHyp":{"meaning":"The scientific prediction you plan to evaluate, written before seeing the result.","write":"State the expected direction/pattern and variables, grounded in prior reasoning.","example":"Before collecting data, predict that dishes assigned 100 mM NaCl will have a lower mean Day 7 shoot length per planted seed than dishes assigned 0 mM NaCl.","questions":["Which exact outcome, units and contrast are specified by the prediction?","What checked evidence or plausible mechanism supports the direction?"],"avoid":"Do not write the observed result after the fact as if it was predicted."},"nullHyp":{"meaning":"The null model/hypothesis associated with a planned inferential test.","write":"State the statistical no-difference/no-association condition appropriate to the analysis.","example":"The mean paired change is 0; or the population correlation is 0.","questions":["What parameter does my test evaluate?","Does the test actually use this null?"],"avoid":"Do not treat the null as “nothing is happening anywhere.”"},"hypReason":{"meaning":"Your explanation for why a formal hypothesis is or is not appropriate.","write":"Connect the decision to the research question and design.","example":"A higher salt concentration might change water availability during seedling development. Check appropriate plant-physiology sources before using this proposed mechanism to justify a lower shoot-length prediction.","questions":["Does checked research support the proposed mechanism or expected direction?","Have you distinguished an unmeasured mechanism from a process actually observed in the study?"],"avoid":"Do not add a hypothesis as a decorative requirement."},"predictorIV":{"meaning":"The variable playing the explanatory role. In experiments it may be a manipulated independent variable; in observational studies it is usually a measured predictor/exposure.","write":"Name the variable and state whether it is assigned/manipulated or merely observed.","example":"Experimental: assigned salinity concentration. Observational: self-reported sleep duration.","questions":["Did I deliberately assign this variable?","If not, should I call it predictor/exposure?"],"avoid":"Do not use IV terminology automatically in observational work."},"outcomeDV":{"meaning":"The response measured to answer the question.","write":"Name the exact outcome, including units or coding when known.","example":"Dish-level mean Day 7 shoot length per planted seed, in centimetres, combining visible-shoot presence with shoot length under a predefined zero rule; missing observations remain marked as missing.","questions":["Which observation and unit produce this outcome?","What measurement time, units and special coding decisions must be recorded?"],"avoid":"Avoid abstract outcomes with no measurement rule."},"operationalDefs":{"meaning":"The exact rules connecting abstract ideas to observable data.","write":"Write how every central variable is manipulated, measured, scored, or coded, including units/categories.","example":"At the Day 7 observation, code visible_shoot as 1 when a shoot is visible and 0 otherwise. Measure visible shoot length in centimetres; record a planned analysis value of 0 cm for no visible shoot, separately from missing data.","questions":["Could another person apply each observation and coding rule consistently?","Are no-visible-shoot codes and genuinely missing observations kept distinct?"],"avoid":"Do not define a construct using another vague word."},"controlCondition":{"meaning":"The reference group/condition used for comparison, if the design needs one.","write":"Name the comparison and why it is the reference.","example":"Dishes assigned the no-added-salt solution, 0 mM NaCl, provide the reference for the 50 and 100 mM treatment dishes.","questions":["What does the other condition get compared with?","Is a formal control actually necessary for this design?"],"avoid":"Do not confuse a control group with keeping procedures constant."},"controlledConditions":{"meaning":"Procedures or environmental features kept similar across conditions.","write":"List influential procedures or environmental features that can reasonably be standardised, and document sources of variation that cannot be kept identical.","example":"Use the same seed batch, substrate, solution volume, light schedule, temperature range and Day 7 measurement rule across the assigned dishes.","questions":["What else could change the outcome?","Which of those factors can I standardize?"],"avoid":"Do not claim a human characteristic is 'controlled' when it is merely measured or varies naturally."},"confounders":{"meaning":"Alternative factors that could explain an observed relationship or create group differences.","write":"Name plausible factors and how the design will prevent, balance, measure, or acknowledge them.","example":"Prior achievement may relate to both study time and exam score; measure it if appropriate or acknowledge it as a limitation.","questions":["What could cause both X and Y?","What differs between groups besides the intended factor?"],"avoid":"Do not list random irrelevant variables."},"experimentalUnit":{"meaning":"In an experiment, the unit independently assigned to a treatment; in observational or qualitative research, the observed case or source unit may differ from the level of individual records.","write":"For an experiment, identify the independently assigned unit and any subsamples. For observational, qualitative or synthesis research, name the cases, records or underlying studies and explain any repetition or nesting.","example":"For the illustrative salinity experiment, each of the 18 dishes is independently assigned to a treatment. Its five seeds supply nested observations, not five independent treatment replicates.","questions":["Which entity independently receives an assigned treatment, if assignment occurs?","Do repeated measurements, excerpts or reports come from the same underlying unit?"],"avoid":"Do not automatically use the number of rows as n."},"population":{"meaning":"The people, organisms, records or system to which the research question and eventual conclusions are intended to apply.","write":"State the population at the scope you actually intend to discuss.","example":"The target population for a proposed school study could be students enrolled in the school's secondary biology courses. One available class would be a narrower recruitment source.","questions":["Which group or system is the question intended to describe?","Does the available sample support a claim about that whole population?"],"avoid":"Do not write 'everyone' when you are studying one accessible class."},"sample":{"meaning":"The actual units contributing evidence to the study, distinguished from any number proposed or eligible before collection.","write":"For a proposal, state the planned or eligible units and any approvals still needed. For completed work, report the actual included and analysed units.","example":"A hypothetical biology class may have up to 30 eligible students, subject to consent. In a separate proposed salinity experiment, 18 dishes would be assigned to treatment, with five seed records per dish. Neither is a completed sample count.","questions":["Which units are proposed, recruited, observed or analysed at this stage?","How are nested records distinguished from independent units?"],"avoid":"Do not present a proposed recruitment target or all seed rows as the actual independent analysis sample."},"samplingMethod":{"meaning":"How the sample is selected or recruited.","write":"Name the method and explain the practical mechanism.","example":"Convenience sample from one available class; simple random sample from a complete roster if a genuine random draw is used.","questions":["Was every unit given a known selection chance?","Was the sample chosen because it was accessible?"],"avoid":"Do not say random when you mean convenient."},"sampleLimits":{"meaning":"How unit selection, recruitment and setting constrain generalisation or transfer of the findings.","write":"Identify who or what the sample represents and explain why selection or context restricts wider inference.","example":"Because volunteers from one available class may differ from nonparticipants and other students, their findings should not automatically be generalised to all secondary learners.","questions":["What important groups/settings are not represented?","Could selection into the sample matter?"],"avoid":"Avoid claiming broad representativeness without a design that supports it."},"instrument":{"meaning":"The physical tool, source record or explicit procedure used to generate a measurement or observation.","write":"Name the actual sensor, rubric, survey, test, observation protocol, interview guide, or data source.","example":"Use a millimetre-marked ruler and a defined Day 7 procedure to record visible shoot length from the substrate surface to the shoot tip. Record shoot-presence status separately.","questions":["What instrument or recording procedure produces each value?","Does the instrument's resolution differ from its accuracy or the validity of the interpretation?"],"avoid":"Do not name only the construct or assume that a fine measurement scale proves accuracy."},"measureQuality":{"meaning":"Evidence about whether the recording procedure is sufficiently consistent and whether its values support the intended interpretation.","write":"Describe checks of repeatability, calibration or coding consistency, then explain separately what the measurement represents and what it cannot establish.","example":"Use the same ruler orientation and shoot-length landmarks, repeat selected readings and record ambiguity. A reliable combined visible-shoot-and-length measure still does not independently assess germination.","questions":["Could the procedure itself produce different recorded values for the same observation?","Does the recorded outcome capture the scientific construct you intend to discuss?"],"avoid":"Do not treat repeatable readings as proof that the measurement supports every intended conclusion."},"ethicsIssues":{"meaning":"Any risk, privacy, authority, consent, coercion, environmental, or safety concern created by the method.","write":"List concerns even if you think they can be managed.","example":"Named student grades create identifiable educational records; participant recruitment by a teacher may create perceived pressure.","questions":["Could participation affect grades/status?","Could someone be identified?","Is any harmful exposure assigned?"],"avoid":"Do not assume access means permission."},"ethicsPlan":{"meaning":"How the identified ethics/safety issues will be handled and who must review/approve them.","write":"State specific safeguards and approval route.","example":"Minimise identifiers and restrict access to coded records. When recruiting one's own students, arrange safeguards for voluntary consent and grading independence, and obtain the ethics review and permissions required for the setting before collection.","questions":["Who has authority?","What data are truly necessary?","How can risk be reduced?"],"avoid":"Do not describe teacher review as formal institutional approval unless it actually is."},"materials":{"meaning":"The materials, instruments, software, datasets, forms, or resources needed to carry out the protocol.","write":"List enough detail to reproduce the work, including relevant amounts/specifications where they affect measurement.","example":"A proposed 18-dish salinity study would use 90 radish seeds from one batch, a uniform substrate, three documented NaCl solutions, equal-volume dispensing equipment, a millimetre-marked ruler and a seed-to-dish record sheet.","questions":["Could another researcher reproduce the setup from this list?","Which specifications could affect the result?"],"avoid":"Do not include irrelevant brand details unless they matter scientifically."},"procedure":{"meaning":"The ordered actions used to collect or generate the evidence.","write":"Write the procedure in sequence, including assignment, timing, measurement, recording, and deviation handling.","example":"Randomly assign 18 dishes to the three NaCl conditions, plant five identified seeds in each and apply equal documented solution volumes. On Day 7, record visible-shoot status and measure shoot length, keeping no-visible-shoot zeroes distinct from missing records. Retain dish IDs, follow the prespecified missing-data rule and log deviations.","questions":["Which unit is assigned, if treatments are used?","What exact time and procedure produce each recorded outcome?","How will missing observations, variations and deviations be documented?"],"avoid":"Do not write a vague recipe that leaves important research decisions implicit."},"replication":{"meaning":"How many independent units exist and how many repeated/technical observations are taken within them.","write":"Separate independent replication from repeats/subsamples.","example":"In the proposed design, 18 independently assigned dishes provide six experimental units per salinity condition. Five seeds per dish would yield 90 nested observations, rather than 90 independent treatment replicates.","questions":["How many units actually receive independent treatment assignments?","Which additional records are nested observations or technical repeats?"],"avoid":"Do not count all measurements as independent treatment replicates or assume the planned replication has adequate statistical power."},"methodControls":{"meaning":"Design protections used to reduce alternative explanations or measurement bias.","write":"Name only protections that actually apply and explain how they are implemented.","example":"Use a recorded random sequence to assign the 18 dishes to salinity levels, plan shelf placement independently of growth, standardise solution volumes, and blind the scorer to concentration labels if feasible.","questions":["Which specific threat does each proposed safeguard address?","What evidence will document that it was implemented?"],"avoid":"Do not list 'randomization' unless a random mechanism will be used."},"rowUnit":{"meaning":"The observation represented by one data row, which may be nested within a different unit of treatment assignment.","write":"Write one sentence: 'One row represents…'","example":"One row represents a planted seed's Day 7 visible-shoot observation and length or missingness, linked to its independently assigned dish through dish_id.","questions":["Can several rows refer to the same treatment, participant or case unit?","Which identifiers preserve any nesting or repeated measurements?"],"avoid":"Do not assume one row equals one independent unit."},"columns":{"meaning":"The variables/fields that will be stored in the dataset.","write":"List each column with type and units/categories.","example":"dish_id (dish identifier); seed_id (within-dish identifier); NaCl_mM (assigned concentration); day (days after setup); visible_shoot (0/1); shoot_length_cm (centimetres or prespecified zero); observation_status (recorded/missing); missing_reason (when available).","questions":["Does the data dictionary distinguish the recorded outcome from an analysis code?","Can all rows be linked to the right underlying unit?"],"avoid":"Avoid ambiguous column names such as data1 or result."},"missingRule":{"meaning":"The rule for representing and handling values that are genuinely missing.","write":"State what counts as missing, how it is coded, and whether complete-case or another approach will be used later.","example":"If a Day 7 observation is unavailable, mark it as missing with a reason if known. For a seed observed without a visible shoot, retain that status and use 0 cm only in the prespecified combined outcome.","questions":["Does 0 represent a recorded outcome or missingness?","What rule will be used for dishes with incomplete seed records?"],"avoid":"Do not invent replacement values just to fill blanks."},"exclusionRule":{"meaning":"A rule decided before results are known for excluding units/observations from a particular analysis.","write":"State objective criteria and preserve a log of exclusions.","example":"Exclude measurements only when the sensor recorded an explicit error code; retain biologically extreme but valid values.","questions":["Would I apply the same rule if the result helped my hypothesis?"],"avoid":"Do not remove values because they make p larger."},"analysisIntent":{"meaning":"The planned way you expect to summarize or analyze the data before final results are known.","write":"For quantitative work, identify the quantity to estimate and the likely analysis family. For qualitative research or a review, describe the interpretive or synthesis approach.","example":"Estimate the difference in mean Day 7 shoot length per planted seed between the 0 and 100 mM dishes, using one prespecified outcome per independently assigned dish. Describe the 50 mM group separately unless further comparisons were planned.","questions":["Which quantity or interpretation directly answers the main question?","Does the proposed analysis respect repeated, paired or nested records?"],"avoid":"Do not choose a method because you expect it to produce a small p-value."},"rawLocation":{"meaning":"Where the original unchanged data file is kept.","write":"Give a specific storage location or naming convention that protects the raw record.","example":"Example storage plan: an approved, access-restricted folder with an unchanged read-only file named raw_data_original.csv. Record the actual location used.","questions":["Could I reconstruct what the original data looked like?"],"avoid":"Do not overwrite the only raw file with cleaned values."},"missingObserved":{"meaning":"Which data are missing and how the analysis handled them.","write":"Describe amount/pattern and actual handling.","example":"Hypothetical data record: 2 of 30 students lacked follow-up data, leaving 28 complete pairs for a paired analysis. Record the actual numbers and handling.","questions":["How many values/units are missing?","Could missingness affect interpretation?"],"avoid":"Do not say 'none' unless checked."},"errorsCorrections":{"meaning":"Any data error, correction, exclusion, or protocol deviation discovered after collection.","write":"Keep an audit trail of original value, corrected value, reason, and decision.","example":"Hypothetical correction log: row 14 originally recorded 55.0 cm; the source sheet showed 5.5 cm. Keep both values and document the source-based correction.","questions":["Can I prove why the correction was made?"],"avoid":"Never silently rewrite inconvenient values."},"plots":{"meaning":"Visual displays used to understand distribution, relationships, or repeated structure before inference.","write":"Name the plot and what you inspected.","example":"Plot each dish-level mean Day 7 shoot length against the three assigned salinity levels. A separate dish-level display of visible-shoot proportions can help examine the combined outcome.","questions":["Can readers see individual independent units and their variation?","Would a secondary plot reveal a different aspect of the recorded phenomenon?"],"avoid":"Do not use a bar chart automatically for every numerical comparison."},"descriptives":{"meaning":"Summaries describing the observed data before or alongside inference.","write":"Choose summaries suited to the evidence and unit of analysis. Report the number of independent cases, as well as linked observations or source records when relevant.","example":"For each salinity group, report analysed dishes, the mean and spread of dish-level outcomes, and observed visible-shoot counts or proportions. Document incomplete observations.","questions":["Which summary is appropriate for the outcome type?","Are independent units distinct from seed or repeated-observation counts?"],"avoid":"Do not calculate means for arbitrary category labels or conceal linked observations behind an inflated sample count."},"primaryEstimand":{"meaning":"For quantitative research, the particular population quantity or contrast to estimate. In mixed-methods work, this target belongs to the numerical strand.","write":"Describe the quantitative target, its population or setting, the comparison or condition and the analysis unit before naming a test. Explain the qualitative strand's interpretive question separately in the analysis plan.","example":"The primary target is the population mean difference in dish-level Day 7 shoot length per planted seed between 0 and 100 mM NaCl conditions.","questions":["What quantity, unit, target population and contrast define the aim?","Which research questions require qualitative interpretation rather than a numerical estimand?"],"avoid":"Do not write only the name of a statistical test."},"analysisChoice":{"meaning":"The method selected to analyse the actual evidence, with a justification matched to the question, units and outcome.","write":"For quantitative work, justify the statistical model, units and assumptions. For qualitative or review-based work, describe how evidence will be interpreted or synthesised.","example":"A Welch two-sample t-test could compare the six independent dish-level means in the 0 and 100 mM groups if its assumptions are defensible. The model must not treat the thirty seeds in each group as independent treatment replicates.","questions":["Does the method fit the outcome and independent-unit structure?","Which diagnostic, credibility or synthesis checks are necessary?","What limitations remain even if the method runs successfully?"],"avoid":"Do not choose a method for its p-value or mistake a test's availability for proof of its suitability."},"assumptionChecks":{"meaning":"The study-design requirements and observed-data features relevant to interpreting the proposed analysis.","write":"Describe what the design already establishes, which diagnostics you would inspect, and what the actual observations show once the data are available.","example":"For a possible dish-level Welch comparison, verify independent assignments and inspect the six dish outcomes per group for unusual values or strong asymmetry. With so few units, a normality test cannot establish the assumed distribution.","questions":["Which condition is established by design, and which requires observed data?","What can a limited number of units actually tell you about the assumption?"],"avoid":"Do not report an assumption as passed before examining its relevant evidence."},"effectSizePlan":{"meaning":"For quantitative results, how you will report magnitude and uncertainty when appropriate, beyond statistical significance alone.","write":"For quantitative analysis, name the raw effect or association and a standardised effect only if useful. Qualitative findings do not require a numerical effect size.","example":"For the 0 versus 100 mM comparison, report the raw difference in mean dish-level Day 7 shoot length in centimetres, with a confidence interval if the method and its assumptions justify one. A standardised effect is optional.","questions":["Which magnitude can readers interpret in a meaningful unit?","What uncertainty statement is supported by the design and estimation procedure?"],"avoid":"Do not treat p-value as an effect size."},"multiplicity":{"meaning":"How you will handle several statistical tests or exploratory comparisons.","write":"Distinguish the prespecified primary contrast from additional analyses. Label exploratory comparisons and justify any multiplicity adjustment where relevant.","example":"Prespecify the 0 versus 100 mM dish comparison. Describe the 50 mM group separately; if additional confirmatory comparisons are planned, define how their multiplicity will be handled.","questions":["Which contrast is the main answer?","Which analyses were defined only after examining the data?"],"avoid":"Do not hide the number of tests performed or describe post-hoc comparisons as prespecified."},"analysisSample":{"meaning":"The actual units and records included in a particular analysis after checking missingness and exclusions.","write":"Report the included independent units and any linked observations; explain discrepancies from planned, recruited or collected counts.","example":"The design planned 18 dishes. Report [verified number] analysed dishes and their assigned groups after checking Day 7 records; do not substitute the ninety planned seed records for the independent analysis sample.","questions":["Which analysed unit count follows from actual records rather than the plan?","Which exclusions or incomplete observations explain the difference?"],"avoid":"Do not silently omit excluded units or report planned sample sizes as completed ones."},"result1":{"meaning":"The main finding answering the research question, traceable to an analysis output, table, figure, source excerpt, or documented case as appropriate.","write":"Report the estimate, pattern, or evidence-grounded interpretation, and identify the source that supports it.","example":"Illustrative Results template. The 0 versus 100 mM dish-level contrast estimated [actual mean difference] cm, with [uncertainty if justified]. Link the estimate to the verified analysis output and figure.","questions":["Where does the exact numerical result or source-supported theme come from?","Does this evidence directly address the primary question?"],"avoid":"Do not invent estimates or make a causal claim broader than the design and outcome warrant."},"result2":{"meaning":"A secondary result that is relevant but not the main answer to the research question.","write":"Report it with the same neutral, traceable style as the primary result and label exploratory analyses when appropriate.","example":"If prespecified as secondary, report the observed proportion of seeds with visible shoots within each dish, with dish-level summaries by salinity group. Identify the actual table and distinguish planned from exploratory comparisons.","questions":["Was visible-shoot presence planned as a secondary outcome?","Which source records and denominators support its reported value?"],"avoid":"Do not relabel visible-shoot emergence as measured germination or promote an unplanned finding to the main result."},"unexpected":{"meaning":"A null, weak, contradictory, or surprising result that still needs transparent reporting.","write":"State it neutrally and exactly.","example":"Hypothetical uncertain result: the estimated difference was imprecise, with an interval spanning effects in both directions. Report the estimate and interval from your own analysis.","questions":["Was this analysis planned or exploratory?","Does uncertainty prevent a strong no-effect claim?"],"avoid":"Do not omit a result because it does not support the hypothesis."},"resultsDraft":{"meaning":"The working Results section of the paper.","write":"Organise the Results or Findings around the research question, using verified numerical outputs, source excerpts or records as appropriate. Identify independent units, denominators and uncertainty where needed.","example":"For quantitative work, introduce the analysed sample and descriptive results before the primary analysis. For qualitative work, introduce the corpus and organise findings around the research question, supported by specific evidence.","questions":["Can each count, estimate or interpretation be traced to the source evidence?","Does the section develop the findings coherently and follow the required genre's boundaries for interpretation?"],"avoid":"Avoid unsupported causal explanation or a rigid one-paragraph-per-result pattern. Qualitative Findings may include interpretation grounded in the evidence."},"directAnswer":{"meaning":"An evidence-supported answer to the research question, stated at the level of certainty the study permits.","write":"Answer using the strongest language the design supports.","example":"For the illustrative salinity study, answer the 0 versus 100 mM contrast only after checking the observed difference, its direction and uncertainty. Claims outside the tested seed batch and growing conditions remain limited.","questions":["What has the actual analysis established about the primary question?","Which aspects of the question remain unresolved by these data?"],"avoid":"Do not present a conditional example as an observed finding or use universal causal language without a design that supports it."},"interpret1":{"meaning":"Your explanation of what the first anchor finding may mean scientifically or practically.","write":"Connect the exact result to a plausible interpretation while preserving uncertainty.","example":"If higher-salinity dishes show lower mean Day 7 shoot length, reduced external water availability or ionic stress may be plausible explanations. Neither process is directly measured by the shoot outcomes.","questions":["Which actual recorded finding motivates the proposed interpretation?","What unmeasured mechanism or alternative explanation prevents a stronger inference?"],"avoid":"Do not present a plausible explanation as if the study directly measured the mechanism."},"interpret2":{"meaning":"Your interpretation of another important finding, especially one that adds complexity or qualification.","write":"Explain the second finding and how it changes the overall answer.","example":"If dish-level visible-shoot proportions are similar but combined mean shoot length differs, the lengths of visible shoots might contribute to that difference. A separate analysis of those shoots would need careful denominators and cannot itself establish a germination mechanism.","questions":["How does this secondary observation qualify the main interpretation?","Which distinct measurements would be required to test the explanation?"],"avoid":"Do not describe an uncollected germination measure as an observed result or ignore contradictory evidence."},"litCompare":{"meaning":"How your results compare with specific prior research.","write":"State where your findings are consistent, different, or difficult to compare, and use design/method details to explain why.","example":"In a fictional comparison, two studies report growth outcomes under different salinity conditions. Before calling the findings consistent, check the original sources, their species, outcome definitions and actual effect estimates.","questions":["Have the cited studies been checked against their original reports?","Can their outcomes, populations and measures be compared meaningfully?"],"avoid":"Do not invent source agreement or write that a finding 'agrees with the literature' without specifying the evidence and limits."},"alternatives":{"meaning":"Other plausible explanations for the observed pattern besides your preferred interpretation.","write":"Name realistic alternatives connected to the design.","example":"Students who sleep more may also have different study routines or workloads; reverse direction is also possible.","questions":["What unmeasured factor could explain both variables?","Could the direction of influence be reversed?"],"avoid":"Do not list implausible possibilities merely to fill space."},"limitations":{"meaning":"Specific design/measurement/sampling limits and how each restricts the conclusion.","write":"For each limitation, state the mechanism and the exact claim it weakens.","example":"For an illustrative one-class sleep study, self-reporting could distort the estimated association, while convenience sampling would restrict generalisation.","questions":["How could this feature bias or limit the result?","What claim becomes weaker because of it?"],"avoid":"Avoid generic 'small sample is a limitation' without explaining why."},"implications":{"meaning":"Reasonable actions or interpretations supported by the evidence and its limits.","write":"State who/what the implication applies to and avoid claiming more than the study showed.","example":"If a verified local observational result suggests an association between sleep and quiz scores, it may guide further questions in that setting. The association alone would not justify prescribing sleep changes to students.","questions":["Who could responsibly use the verified finding, and for what purpose?","Which stronger decision would require additional evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not treat statistical significance, a compelling quotation or a local finding as automatic justification for a broad recommendation."},"future":{"meaning":"A specific next study that addresses a limitation or unanswered mechanism.","write":"Propose a design improvement directly connected to current evidence.","example":"A follow-up radish experiment could use more independently assigned dishes across seed batches and record radicle emergence with a separate germination criterion. Shoot length could then be examined under its own prespecified analysis plan.","questions":["Which limitation would the additional observations address?","What would the new measure permit that the existing data cannot show?"],"avoid":"Avoid vague 'more research is needed.'"},"discussionDraft":{"meaning":"The Discussion's connected interpretation of the findings, relevant scholarship, limitations and unanswered questions.","write":"Develop the scientific interpretation from the actual evidence. Bring in checked literature, alternative explanations and limitations where they clarify or change the argument, allowing the findings to guide paragraph order.","example":"Begin with the supported result or question it helps answer, then develop the reasoning through the specific evidence. A limitation belongs beside the inference it constrains, while a literature comparison should explain the relevance of the cited studies.","questions":["How does each sentence develop, qualify or test the preceding interpretation?","Which claims are grounded in observed results, and where does uncertainty change the conclusion?"],"avoid":"Do not repeat Results with added adjectives, impose a rigid paragraph pattern or rely on formulaic transitions in place of substantive connections."},"conclusionDraft":{"meaning":"The closing answer to the research question when findings exist, or the bounded purpose of work still proposed.","write":"For completed work, state the verified answer and its main inference limit without adding new findings. For a proposal, close with the planned inquiry and expected contribution without asserting results.","example":"If a completed dish-level experiment shows a lower combined Day 7 shoot-length outcome under higher salinity, its conclusion should report the observed estimate and uncertainty. A proposal could instead close with the intended comparison and its measurement limits.","questions":["Does the conclusion answer only what the completed evidence can support?","Have proposed steps been kept separate from observed results?"],"avoid":"Do not introduce new results, claim that a proposal found an effect, or make the conclusion more certain than the analysis permits."},"abstractDraft":{"meaning":"A stand-alone summary reflecting what has actually been completed or proposed, usually drafted once the main paper is stable.","write":"For a completed empirical study, summarise the purpose, method and sample or system, principal findings, and bounded conclusion. For proposals, describe the planned work without inventing results; use the structure required for other research genres.","example":"Completed-study template: purpose, method, principal result, and bounded conclusion. Proposal template: research purpose, planned approach, expected contribution, and current uncertainties.","questions":["Can readers tell whether the work is completed or proposed, and which findings are actually available?","Is there any calculation detail that belongs in Results instead?"],"avoid":"For a completed empirical study, include the actual findings. Do not manufacture results for a proposal."},"titleDraft":{"meaning":"A specific, accurate title that helps a reader know what was studied.","write":"Name the central variables/phenomenon and useful context/design information.","example":"Reported Sleep Duration and Biology Quiz Scores in a Secondary Classroom","questions":["Does the title imply causation the design cannot support?","Can I remove vague words such as 'A Study of'?"],"avoid":"Avoid sensational or causal titles for observational evidence."},"keywords":{"meaning":"A small set of searchable terms that describe the central topic, variables, population/context, or method.","write":"Choose commonly used research terms, usually several concise phrases.","example":"retrieval practice; biology education; delayed recall; secondary students","questions":["Would a researcher search this phrase to find my paper?","Are these terms more informative than words already obvious from 'research' or 'study'?"],"avoid":"Do not use full sentences or extremely general terms such as science, data, and research."},"citationStyle":{"meaning":"The formatting system required by your teacher, journal, or discipline.","write":"Select the required style and follow its official rules consistently.","example":"APA 7 if your course instructions require APA seventh edition.","questions":["What does the assignment explicitly require?","Did the teacher provide a special local format?"],"avoid":"Do not choose a style based on personal preference when the assignment specifies one."},"citationAudit":{"meaning":"A list of citation/reference problems that still require checking against actual sources.","write":"Record missing matches, incomplete bibliographic data, unsupported claims, or formatting issues.","example":"Suppose a draft cites the fictional reference (Garcia, 2022) without a source record. Locate and verify a real source before keeping the claim or citation.","questions":["Does each citation to a recoverable source have the appropriate reference-list entry, with style-specific exceptions handled correctly?","Does the source support the exact nearby claim?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a DOI or missing author information."},"finalEdits":{"meaning":"The final methodological and writing changes needed before submission.","write":"List specific corrections with the section or sentence affected, the source evidence needed and a way to verify the change.","example":"Check the independent dish count in Methods and Results, correct any wording that calls visible-shoot presence germination, verify source-to-claim citations and reread adjoining paragraphs for logical continuity.","questions":["Which sentence, figure or table still cannot be traced to a reliable record?","After the correction, do the neighbouring sentences and related sections remain consistent?"],"avoid":"Do not record only 'proofread' or mark an unresolved claim as verified."}},"labs":{"literature":{"search":{"title":"Search log and search coverage","text":"Record exactly where and how you searched. The log documents the sources and search procedures used, so claims about missing literature remain bounded by the evidence actually retrieved.","terms":["boolean search","literature gap"]},"sources":{"title":"Source records and their contributions","text":"Record the source’s design and context alongside its measures and findings. Document relevant limitations or quality concerns so evidence can be compared later.","terms":["TRAAP","observational study","measurement validity"],"decisionKeys":["sourceType"]},"screen":{"title":"Screening against stated criteria","text":"Include or exclude sources using prespecified relevance/design criteria. Do not keep a paper because its result agrees with your expectation.","terms":["literature gap","citation"],"decisionKeys":["screeningStatus"]},"study":{"title":"Study matrix and meaningful comparison","text":"Read across rows to compare populations, methods, measures, and findings. Differences in methods may help explain apparent disagreement, but the explanation must be checked against the studies.","terms":["synthesis","measurement validity"]},"synth":{"title":"Synthesising evidence across studies","text":"A theme should connect multiple sources and also record conflicting or mixed evidence. The question is 'what does the evidence collectively support, and under what conditions?'","terms":["synthesis","theme","generalization"],"decisionKeys":["themeStance"]},"gap":{"title":"A defensible study rationale","text":"A legitimate project can be replication, extension, local investigation, method comparison, or exploration of inconsistent findings. Global novelty is not required.","terms":["literature gap","replication","generalization"]},"claims":{"title":"Checking literature claims against evidence","text":"Trace each substantive claim to the sources that support it. Where those studies differ in population, measurement or design, describe the relevant boundary.","terms":["citation","causal claim","generalization"]},"outline":{"title":"Paragraph planning through connected ideas","text":"Let each paragraph develop an idea about the literature through relevant evidence. Introduce comparisons and qualifications where they clarify the reasoning, and connect the paragraph naturally to the wider question.","terms":["synthesis","theme"]}},"methods":{"design":{"title":"Choosing a design that fits the question","text":"Determine whether the main factor is assigned or observed and whether units are measured repeatedly. Use these design features to identify what conclusions the evidence could reasonably support.","terms":["experimental unit","observational study","random assignment","causal claim","paired data"],"decisionKeys":["mdAssignment","mdSameUnit"]},"vars":{"title":"Defining variables and constructs","text":"A construct such as stress or growth needs a role and an operational definition. Design determines whether 'independent variable' is appropriate terminology.","terms":["construct","operational definition","independent variable","predictor","outcome"]},"rep":{"title":"Identifying units and independent replication","text":"For experiments, identify the units independently assigned to treatments. In observational research, identify the observed units and any clustering or repeated measurements. Distinguish technical readings and subsamples to avoid inflating the number of independent observations.","terms":["experimental unit","replication","technical replicate","subsample","pseudoreplication","repeated measures"]},"sample":{"title":"Sampling and the scope of conclusions","text":"Sampling affects the populations or settings to which findings may apply. Random sampling concerns how units enter a study, while random assignment concerns which treatment they receive.","terms":["population","sample","sampling frame","random sampling","random assignment","generalization"],"decisionKeys":["msMethod"]},"measure":{"title":"Making measurements interpretable","text":"Explain how each construct is measured or coded, with the procedure, scale and timing clearly defined. Consider reliability and validity, and document relevant calibration or scoring checks.","terms":["reliability","measurement validity","calibration","scale","binary","ordinal"]},"ethics":{"title":"Ethics, safety and required permissions","text":"Access to classmates, grades, recordings, health information or interventions does not itself authorise their use in research. Before collecting data, identify the relevant risks, consent requirements, privacy protections and approval process.","terms":["identifiable data"],"decisionKeys":["meHuman","meMinors","meIdent","meSensitive","meIntervention"]},"procedure":{"title":"Writing a reproducible procedure","text":"Write ordered actions, what is recorded, and what counts as a deviation. Another researcher should not have to invent missing decisions.","terms":["protocol","blinding","counterbalancing","random assignment"]},"data":{"title":"Defining data and evidence records","text":"For tabular data, define what one row represents and document column IDs, units, coding and missing-value rules. For interviews, field notes or documents, define the evidence records and how they will remain traceable.","terms":["data dictionary","identifier","binary","categorical","ordinal","numerical","missing data"]},"audit":{"title":"Checking and preserving the research plan","text":"Resolve essential design and ethics issues before proceeding. For prospective work, preserve the plan made before viewing outcomes. For completed work, document when decisions were actually made and identify retrospective changes.","terms":["protocol","experimental unit","measurement validity","generalization"]}},"data":{"import":{"title":"Importing and preserving raw evidence","text":"Use a copy for analysis and keep the original file unchanged elsewhere. Stable IDs are essential for paired, repeated, or nested observations.","terms":["identifier","paired data","repeated measures","missing data"]},"quality":{"title":"Investigating data quality before exclusion","text":"Missing values, duplicates, unusual values, and coding problems are reasons to inspect the source and apply documented rules. They are not automatic deletion commands.","terms":["missing data","outlier","data dictionary"]},"explore":{"title":"Exploring the structure of the evidence","text":"Choose plots that reveal the relevant numerical distributions or relationships, and summarise categorical data with counts and proportions. For paired or repeated observations, preserve links between measurements from the same unit.","terms":["numerical","categorical","binary","paired data","descriptive statistics"]},"setup":{"title":"Choosing the quantitative analysis target","text":"For quantitative analysis, identify the quantity to estimate and the outcome type. Check whether observations are independent, paired or clustered before choosing a method.","terms":["estimand","binary","ordinal","nominal","independent groups","paired data"],"decisionKeys":["analysisStructure","outcomeType"]},"run":{"title":"Matching analysis columns to the design","text":"The same test name can be wrong if the columns do not represent the planned units. Confirm what each selected column means before running.","terms":["predictor","outcome","paired data","p-value","effect size","confidence interval"],"decisionKeys":["correlationCoefficient","anovaModel","fisherChoice"]},"history":{"title":"Recording analyses and decisions","text":"Store the primary analysis with its variable mapping, assumptions, estimate, uncertainty, and warnings. Do not choose a stored run merely because it has the smallest p-value.","terms":["p-value","effect size","confidence interval","inference"]},"audit":{"title":"Checking results against the research question","text":"A defensible result answers the same question the study was designed to answer and preserves the true unit structure.","terms":["estimand","experimental unit","generalization"]}},"writing":{"intro":{"title":"Developing the introduction and research question","text":"Introduce the question through relevant context and research evidence. Explain how the available literature and the limits of the proposed study shape a defensible rationale, without claiming novelty that has not been established.","terms":["construct","synthesis","literature gap"]},"lit":{"title":"Connecting ideas across the literature","text":"Organise the review around ideas or meaningful comparisons among studies. Use source-specific summaries where they help the argument, and show how the evidence fits together.","terms":["synthesis","theme","citation"]},"method":{"title":"Reporting methods with fidelity to the research","text":"For completed research, describe what was actually done and disclose any changes from the planned protocol. For a proposal, describe intended procedures as plans rather than completed actions.","terms":["protocol","operational definition","experimental unit","observational unit"]},"results":{"title":"Presenting findings with appropriate interpretation","text":"Report estimates, patterns or interpretations supported by the evidence, with clear links to analysis records or source excerpts. When sections are separate, broader explanations and implications generally belong in Discussion; qualitative Findings may also contain grounded interpretation.","terms":["effect size","confidence interval","p-value","theme"]},"discussion":{"title":"Interpreting findings within their limits","text":"Develop interpretations from the findings that answer the question. Discuss alternative explanations and relevant literature where they clarify the reasoning, and show how limitations restrict the conclusions.","terms":["causal claim","confounder","generalization"]},"closing":{"title":"Writing conclusions and accurate abstracts","text":"The conclusion returns to the question and the claims supported by the study. For completed empirical work, the abstract summarises the purpose, method, findings and conclusion; a proposal should describe planned work without inventing results.","terms":["generalization","effect size"]},"citations":{"title":"Verifying citations and their supporting claims","text":"A citation must correspond to a real source and the source must support the nearby claim at the stated scope.","terms":["citation","generalization"]},"audit":{"title":"Checking the paper's evidence and reasoning","text":"Trace the argument from its research question through the selected evidence, method and analysis to the final claims. Check that each step supports the next, whether the evidence consists of measurements, documents or qualitative records.","terms":["protocol","estimand","causal claim","synthesis"]}}},"choices":{"questionType":{"title":"How do I choose the question family?","prompt":"Choose based on the kind of answer you need, not which label sounds most scientific.","options":{"Descriptive":"Choose when you want to describe what exists, how common something is, or how values are distributed. Example: What proportion of seeds germinate at each salinity?","Correlational / observational":"Choose when key variables are measured as they naturally occur and you want to examine a relationship. Example: Is sleep duration associated with quiz score?","Experimental":"Choose when the researcher deliberately assigns/manipulates a condition and the design is built to estimate an effect. Manipulation alone is not enough; assignment and validity protections still matter.","Qualitative":"Choose when you want to understand experiences, meanings, processes, explanations, or perspectives using evidence such as interviews, open responses, or observations.","Literature review":"Choose when your evidence will come from synthesizing existing studies and you are not collecting new participant/experimental data.","Meta-analysis":"Choose only for a quantitative review in which sufficiently comparable effect estimates from multiple studies can be combined using an explicit statistical model. This is an advanced route.","Mixed methods / unsure":"Mixed methods means deliberately integrating quantitative and qualitative evidence to answer connected parts of the same problem. Choose unsure if you genuinely have not resolved the design yet."},"questions":["Am I collecting new data or synthesizing existing studies?","Will I assign/manipulate the key factor?","Am I asking about a relationship, an effect, a description, or an experience/process?"]},"gapType":{"title":"What kind of study rationale do I actually have?","prompt":"Use the smallest claim the literature can support.","options":{"Documented literature gap":"Use when a sufficiently broad, documented search shows an important question, population, measure, or relationship has not been adequately addressed. Do not use from a quick search.","Replication":"Use when you intentionally repeat a prior design/question to see whether a result can be reproduced under similar conditions.","Extension to a new context/population":"Use when prior work exists and you study the question in a meaningfully different population, setting, measure, or condition.","Method comparison":"Use when the project compares methods, instruments, scoring approaches, or procedures.","Resolve or explore inconsistent findings":"Use when credible studies disagree or vary and your study investigates a plausible reason or adds evidence to that uncertainty.","Local/practical question":"Use when the project addresses a real local decision or context even if the broad topic is already well studied.","Learning-focused investigation":"Use when the primary purpose is a carefully bounded educational investigation of research reasoning/methods.","Other":"Use when none fits, then explain the rationale precisely."},"questions":["Do I truly know something is absent from the literature?","Would replication or extension describe the project more accurately?","Is the value mainly local or practical?"]},"designType":{"title":"How do I choose the primary design?","prompt":"Start with what the researcher actually does to generate evidence.","options":{"Descriptive":"Measures or documents a phenomenon without a primary relationship/effect comparison.","Correlational / observational":"Measures predictors/exposures and outcomes without assigning the key exposure.","Experimental":"Researcher assigns/manipulates conditions with design protections aimed at estimating a causal effect.","Quasi-experimental":"An intervention or policy change is evaluated without full random assignment, using an appropriate comparison or time structure. Simply comparing existing groups does not make a study quasi-experimental; causal claims still require care.","Qualitative":"Uses systematic non-numerical evidence to understand meanings, experiences, processes, or explanations.","Mixed methods":"Intentionally integrates quantitative and qualitative evidence, with a reason for how the strands answer connected questions.","Literature review":"Synthesises existing research using explicit methods suited to the question. Some reviews include meta-analysis; others use qualitative or non-pooled synthesis.","Meta-analysis":"Quantitatively combines sufficiently comparable study estimates using a meta-analytic model, ordinarily as part of a systematic review.","Undecided":"Use temporarily if important design information is still unresolved."},"questions":["Is the key factor assigned or observed?","Are the same units measured repeatedly?","What is the strongest claim I need the design to support?"]},"hypothesisNeeded":{"title":"Does my study need a formal hypothesis?","prompt":"A hypothesis is useful when a specific prediction is genuinely part of the study logic.","options":{"Yes":"Choose for a confirmatory study with a prespecified, evidence-based prediction that can be tested by the planned design.","No":"Choose for descriptive, many exploratory qualitative, and some review questions where forcing a prediction would add no scientific value.","Unsure":"Choose while you decide whether the question is confirmatory or exploratory. Explain the reason before locking the method."},"questions":["Am I testing a prediction or first describing/exploring a phenomenon?","Would I write this prediction before seeing the data?"]},"citationStyle":{"title":"Which citation style should I use?","prompt":"This is usually decided by the assignment, discipline, journal, or teacher.","options":{"APA 7":"Use when the assignment requires American Psychological Association seventh edition style.","MLA":"Use when explicitly required by the course/discipline.","Chicago":"Use when explicitly required by the course/discipline.","Other / teacher-specific":"Use when your teacher provides a different format or local instructions."},"questions":["What does the assignment sheet say?","Did the teacher provide a reference example?"]},"mdAssignment":{"title":"How do units reach conditions or exposures?","prompt":"This determines what kind of comparison you really have.","options":{"Random assignment":"A random process places experimental units into conditions.","Nonrandom assignment":"The researcher assigns conditions, but not through a random process.","Naturally occurring / observed exposure":"The researcher does not assign the key exposure; it is measured as it already exists.","Single-group / no assignment":"There is one group/condition or no between-condition assignment.","Not applicable":"Assignment does not make sense for this design, such as many qualitative or literature-review studies."},"questions":["Could the same unit have been assigned to another condition by the researcher?","Was a random mechanism actually used?"]},"mdSameUnit":{"title":"Do the same units appear more than once?","prompt":"This decides whether observations are paired/repeated.","options":{"yes":"Choose if the same person, plant, animal, sample, classroom, or other unit contributes observations across multiple times or conditions.","no":"Choose when distinct units contribute one observation each for the comparison. If a unit is measured repeatedly across times or conditions, choose yes.","not applicable":"Choose if repeated-condition logic does not apply to the design."},"questions":["Can I match observation A and B to the same underlying unit?","Does one ID appear in several conditions or times?"]},"msMethod":{"title":"How was the sample selected?","prompt":"Use the actual mechanism, not the method you wish you had.","options":{"Census / all available units":"You include every unit in the defined accessible frame. This is a census of that frame and does not necessarily represent a broader target population.","Simple random / probability sample":"Use for a documented probability design. In simple random sampling, units have equal selection probabilities; other probability methods may differ, so record the actual procedure.","Stratified probability sample":"Units are divided into strata, then probability sampled within strata.","Cluster sample":"Groups or clusters are selected as sampling units. Explain how clusters were chosen and which units within them were studied; probability sampling requires a probability-based selection process.","Systematic sample":"Units are selected at a fixed interval from an ordered frame. For probability-based systematic sampling, use a random starting position and check whether the ordering could bias selection.","Convenience sample":"Units enter because they are easy/available to access.","Purposive / criterion sample":"Units are deliberately selected because they meet criteria important to the research question, common in qualitative work.","Snowball / network sample":"Existing participants or contacts help identify additional participants.","Other":"Describe the exact selection mechanism."},"questions":["Was there a real probability mechanism?","Was access/convenience the main reason these units entered?"]},"meHuman":{"title":"Does this involve human participants or linkable human records?","prompt":"Answer yes if the project interacts with people for research data or uses records that can be linked to them.","options":{"yes":"Includes surveys, interviews, observations of identifiable people, student records, health/behavior data, or interventions involving people.","no":"No human participants and no identifiable/linkable human records are used."},"questions":["Could the data be connected to a person directly or indirectly?"]},"meMinors":{"title":"Are any participants under the applicable age of legal adulthood?","prompt":"Minors often require additional permission/assent safeguards.","options":{"yes":"At least one participant is a minor.","no":"All participants are adults under the applicable policy.","not applicable":"There are no human participants."},"questions":["What age/policy rules apply in the actual institution?"]},"meIdent":{"title":"Could the data identify or be linked back to a person?","prompt":"Identification can be direct or indirect.","options":{"yes":"Names, IDs, faces, voices, precise location, contact information, or linkable records are collected/retained.","no":"The planned data do not contain direct identifiers and cannot reasonably be linked back using retained information."},"questions":["Could someone combine fields to identify a participant?","Is a code key retained somewhere?"]},"meSensitive":{"title":"Does the project collect sensitive personal information?","prompt":"Consider whether disclosure could create harm, stigma, disciplinary consequences, or privacy concerns.","options":{"yes":"Includes sensitive health, behaviour, identity, academic, family, financial, or similarly private information under the relevant policy/context.","no":"The information collected is not sensitive under the relevant policy/context."},"questions":["Would a participant reasonably expect this information to stay private?","Could disclosure cause harm?"]},"meIntervention":{"title":"Will the student researcher assign an intervention to people?","prompt":"This includes deliberately changing what participants receive or do for research purposes.","options":{"yes":"The research plan assigns or manipulates a treatment, behaviour, exposure, instruction, substance, or other condition involving people.","no":"The project observes existing conditions/behaviors or uses non-interventional evidence."},"questions":["Am I asking participants to do something they would not otherwise do for the study?","Could the intervention create physical, psychological, academic, or social risk?"]},"sourceType":{"title":"What kind of source is this?","prompt":"Classify what the document actually is because different source types support different claims.","options":{"Peer-reviewed empirical article":"Reports an original empirical analysis. The authors may have collected the data themselves or analysed an existing dataset.","Systematic review / meta-analysis":"Uses explicit methods to identify and synthesize prior studies; meta-analysis also quantitatively pools estimates.","Scholarly review":"Synthesizes prior scholarship but may not use a systematic review protocol.","Government / institutional report":"Issued by a public/institutional body; evaluate methods and purpose rather than assuming quality.","Book / chapter":"Long-form scholarly source; usefulness depends on recency, authorship, evidence, and purpose.","Other":"Classify and evaluate the source explicitly."}},"screeningStatus":{"title":"What does screening status mean?","prompt":"The status records where a source stands under your inclusion/exclusion rules.","options":{"Included":"Meets the current eligibility criteria and remains in the evidence set.","Pending":"Not enough information has been checked yet to decide.","Excluded":"Does not meet a stated eligibility criterion; record the reason."}},"themeStance":{"title":"How does this source relate to the theme?","prompt":"A theme matrix should record disagreement, not erase it.","options":{"supports":"The source provides evidence consistent with the theme claim.","conflicts":"The source provides evidence inconsistent with the theme claim under a reasonably comparable question/measure.","mixed":"The source partly supports and partly complicates the theme, or results vary by outcome/subgroup/condition.","background":"The source provides context/method information but is not direct evidence for or against the theme claim."}},"analysisStructure":{"title":"How do I identify the data/design structure?","prompt":"First identify the outcome type and whether observations are independent, paired or nested. Some structures, such as three or more repeated numerical measurements, need methods beyond the simplified choices below.","options":{"Describe one numerical variable":"One numerical outcome; the goal is to describe its distribution, centre and spread rather than compare groups.","Describe one categorical variable":"One categorical outcome; summarise counts and proportions.","Association between two measured variables":"Two measured variables from the same observational units; goal is association, not assigned treatment effect.","Two independent groups, numerical outcome":"Different independent units in each of two groups; outcome is numerical.","Two paired/repeated numerical measurements":"The same units (or explicitly matched units) supply two related numerical measurements.","Three or more independent groups":"Different independent units across three or more groups; numerical outcome.","Two categorical variables":"Both variables are categorical and observations belong to appropriate independent/paired structure for the selected method.","Paired binary":"Same units measured in two conditions/times; outcome has exactly two categories.","Repeated binary":"Same units measured across three or more conditions/times; outcome has exactly two categories."},"questions":["Does one unit appear more than once?","Does the outcome have numbers, ordered categories, two categories, or named categories?"]},"outcomeType":{"title":"What kind of outcome do I have?","prompt":"Classify what the recorded values mean, not how they happen to look in the spreadsheet.","options":{"Continuous/numerical":"Meaningful numerical values such as height, time, concentration, or a test score. Check whether the measure is continuous, discrete, or an ordinal rating before choosing an analysis.","Count":"Number of events/items, usually nonnegative integers such as colony count.","Ordinal":"Ordered categories or rankings where equal spacing is not guaranteed.","Binary":"Exactly two categories such as yes/no or correct/incorrect.","Nominal categories":"Named categories with no inherent order, such as species or treatment label.","Text/qualitative":"Written, spoken or observational evidence analysed for meanings, patterns or themes."}},"correlationCoefficient":{"title":"Pearson or Spearman?","prompt":"Do not choose only from a normality test.","options":{"Pearson r":"Describes linear association between two numerical variables; inspect the scatterplot for linear form and influential points.","Spearman ρ":"Describes monotonic rank association and is often useful with ordinal data or when a monotonic relationship is more appropriate than a linear one."},"questions":["What does the scatterplot look like?","Are the variables numerical/ordinal and is a linear relationship sensible?"]},"anovaModel":{"title":"Welch or classical one-way ANOVA?","prompt":"Both compare mean structure across independent groups, but their variance assumptions differ.","options":{"Welch ANOVA":"Generally preferable when group variances/sample sizes differ and a one-way independent-group mean comparison is appropriate.","Classical ANOVA":"Uses the classical equal-variance one-way model; use only when that model is justified."}},"fisherChoice":{"title":"Chi-square or Fisher exact in a 2×2 table?","prompt":"Sparse expected counts can make chi-square approximations less reliable.","options":{"Chi-square":"An approximate test for association between categorical variables, using independent counts when expected-count conditions are adequate. Paired observations require a paired method.","Fisher exact":"An exact method for an independent 2×2 count table, especially useful with sparse counts. Matched or paired binary data require a different analysis."}}},"novicePromise":"You are not expected to know research terminology before you begin. When a term or choice appears, the site should explain what it means, how to recognize it in your own project, and what a reasonable example looks like. If a screen still leaves you unsure, use Student Guide or Research Terms before guessing."};